(window.COURSE_MODULES = window.COURSE_MODULES || []).push({
n: 18, hours: 10, title: "Other Scopes in Your Bid: Demo, Grading, Drainage, Hardscape and More",
slides: [

{ t: "cover",
  h: "Other Scopes in Your Bid: Demo, Grading, Drainage, Hardscape and More",
  lead: "Landscape bid packages often hand you more than plants and pipe. Demolition, grading, erosion control, drainage, concrete, furnishings, lighting and general conditions can all land on your scope sheet. This module explains each one: what it is, who normally does it, whether your license covers it, how to take it off, how to price it, and how to exclude it in writing when it isn't yours.",
  objectives: [
    "Read a scope sheet or bid item list and spot every item that isn't irrigation or planting",
    "Check each extra scope against the C-27 classification and know when a C-8, C-10, C-12, C-13, A or other license is needed",
    "Take off and price demolition, haul-off, fine grading, import and export, with swell and shrink math written out",
    "Price erosion control BMPs, landscape drainage, mow curbs, DG paving, boulders and site furnishings",
    "Handle lighting, water meters, backflow, temporary water, DigAlert and potholing without leaving money on the table",
    "Decide for each item: do it, sub it out, or exclude it, and write the proposal language for each choice",
    "Use real line items and prices from Caltrans and City of San Diego jobs to check your numbers"
  ],
  time: [["Reading and slides", "4.5 h"], ["Practice assignments", "5.5 h"], ["Module total", "10 h"]],
  body: `<p>The owner's rule for this division: anything someone might come across in a bid must be explained. This is the module for the items that show up next to your landscape work and catch new estimators by surprise. Keep Module 7 (takeoff), Module 9 (labor) and Module 13 (proposals) open beside it.</p>`
},

{ h: "Why landscape bids carry other scopes",
  lead: "The landscape sub is often the last trade on site and works in the same dirt as grading, drainage and hardscape. So GCs and agencies push nearby work into the landscape package.",
  body: `<ol class="steps">
<li><div><b>The GC's scope sheet</b>A private GC splits the job into bid packages. The “landscape” package may list demo of old planters, fine grading, area drains, mow curbs, DG paths, benches and landscape lighting.</div></li>
<li><div><b>The specs</b>The landscape architect's spec sections (CSI Division 32) may include site furnishings, unit pavers or decomposed granite (DG). Whatever is in “your” spec section, the GC will expect in your price.</div></li>
<li class="hl"><div><b>Public bid item lists</b>On a Caltrans landscape job you may be the prime. Then every item is yours to price or sub out: traffic control, storm water plans, clearing and grubbing, excavation, concrete, asphalt dike, signs and electrical service.</div></li>
<li><div><b>Lump sum public jobs</b>A City of San Diego park job may have one lump sum item that “includes but is not limited to” walkways, seating, irrigation, planting and lighting. The prime then splits it among subs, and you must say exactly which parts you cover.</div></li>
</ol>`,
  ex: `<p>Real example: Caltrans 11-422004 (I-5 Carmel Valley) had 56 bid items. Only about 20 were landscape or irrigation. The rest included Remove Concrete (66 CY), Roadway Excavation (340 CY), Hot Mix Asphalt, Pervious Concrete Pavement, a 6" perforated pipe underdrain (230 LF) and Minor Concrete (19 CY). A landscape company, Diversified Landscape Co., won it at $557,307.25 against three civil contractors.</p>`,
  watch: `<p>The biggest risk is silence. If an item is on the scope sheet and your proposal says nothing about it, the GC will assume it's included. Every extra scope gets one of three answers in writing: included, included by a named sub, or excluded.</p>`,
  src: "Caltrans 11-422004 Bid Item List and Bid Summary (09/09/2021); City of San Diego K-24-1952-DBB-3-A, Section 7-3.1."
},

{ h: "Your license: what the C-27 covers",
  lead: "The C-27 is broad for landscape work, but it has edges. Know the exact words, and know the rule for work outside them.",
  body: `<p><strong>The official C-27 text (16 CCR §832.27):</strong></p>
<p>“A landscape contractor constructs, maintains, repairs, installs, or subcontracts the development of landscape systems and facilities for public and private gardens and other areas which are designed to aesthetically, architecturally, horticulturally, or functionally improve the grounds within or surrounding a structure or a tract or plot of land. In connection therewith, a landscape contractor prepares and grades plots and areas of land for the installation of any architectural, horticultural and decorative treatment or arrangement.”</p>
<div class="twocol">
<div><h3>Clearly inside</h3><ul>
<li>Irrigation, planting, soil prep, mulch</li>
<li>Preparing and grading land <em>for</em> landscape</li>
<li>Landscape “facilities” that improve the grounds: DG paths, edging, boulders, landscape drainage tied to the planting</li>
<li>Subcontracting landscape work to others</li>
</ul></div>
<div><h3>Gray or outside</h3><ul>
<li>Mass grading, street paving, utilities (A or C-12 work)</li>
<li>Structural concrete and large flatwork (C-8)</li>
<li>Line-voltage electrical (C-10)</li>
<li>Pools and spas (C-53), large retaining walls, shade structures that need a building permit</li>
</ul></div>
</div>`,
  deeper: `<p><strong>Incidental and supplemental work.</strong> Business and Professions Code §7059 lets a specialty contractor do work in another trade when it is “incidental and supplemental” to the work it is licensed for. CSLB's regulation (16 CCR §831) says that means work “essential to accomplish the work in which the contractor is classified.” A short trench for a controller conduit inside your irrigation job may qualify. A whole concrete plaza does not. You can always subcontract the other trade to a properly licensed firm.</p>
<p><strong>Your B license.</strong> B&P §7057 says a B contractor can't take a prime contract or a subcontract for one specialty trade (other than framing or carpentry) unless the contract needs at least two unrelated trades, or the B holds that specialty license, or subcontracts the work to a licensed specialty contractor. The company's B helps with multi-trade work; it doesn't turn the landscape division into a concrete contractor.</p>`,
  watch: `<p>When you're not sure, ask CSLB before bid day and keep the answer in the bid file. Working outside your classification can lead to citations and can put your right to be paid at risk. That is a legal question for the owner and the company's attorney, not a guess on bid day.</p>`,
  src: "CSLB C-27 Landscaping classification (16 CCR §832.27): <a href=\"https://www.cslb.ca.gov/About_us/Library/Licensing_Classifications/Licensing_Classifications_Detail.aspx?Class=C27\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">cslb.ca.gov</a>; B classification and B&P §7057 text on the CSLB B page; B&P §7059 and 16 CCR §831 as quoted in CSLB's 2023 rulemaking notice (cslb.ca.gov/Resources/LawsAndRegulations/2023)."
},

{ h: "The other licenses you'll meet",
  lead: "When a scope falls outside the C-27, the bid documents, the GC, or your sub's license tells you who should do it. These are the classes that show up on landscape jobs.",
  body: `<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Class</th><th>Name</th><th>What CSLB says it does (short)</th><th>Shows up on landscape jobs for</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>A</td><td>General Engineering</td><td>Fixed works needing engineering skill, including irrigation, drainage, parks, playgrounds, excavating, grading, trenching, paving</td><td>Prime on civil and park jobs; mass grading, storm drain, streets</td></tr>
<tr><td>C-8</td><td>Concrete</td><td>Forms, pours, places and finishes mass, pavement, flat and other concrete</td><td>Walks, plazas, curbs, footings, seat walls</td></tr>
<tr><td>C-10</td><td>Electrical</td><td>Installs or connects wires, fixtures, raceways, conduits that use electrical energy</td><td>Controller power, pole lights, line-voltage landscape lighting</td></tr>
<tr><td>C-7</td><td>Low Voltage Systems</td><td>Energy-limited systems not over 91 volts, listing “low voltage landscape lighting”</td><td>Low-voltage lighting systems</td></tr>
<tr><td>C-12</td><td>Earthwork and Paving</td><td>Digs, moves and places earth for cut, fill, excavation, grade, trench, backfill; also paving</td><td>Rough grading, import/export, asphalt</td></tr>
<tr><td>C-13</td><td>Fencing</td><td>Fences, railings, game court enclosures, playground game equipment, backstops, gates (not masonry walls)</td><td>Fences, gates, backstops</td></tr>
<tr><td>C-21</td><td>Building Moving/Demolition</td><td>Demolishes and removes structures, including foundations</td><td>Removing buildings or structures</td></tr>
<tr><td>C-29</td><td>Masonry</td><td>Concrete block, brick, stone, including load-bearing and non-load-bearing walls and fences</td><td>Block walls, stone veneer, seat walls</td></tr>
<tr><td>C-42</td><td>Sanitation System</td><td>Septic tanks, storm drains, other drain structures</td><td>Storm drain lines and structures</td></tr>
<tr><td>C-53</td><td>Swimming Pool</td><td>Pools, spas, hot tubs</td><td>Pools and spas (not ordinary fountains)</td></tr>
<tr><td>C-61/D-6</td><td>Concrete-Related Services</td><td>Interlocking precast pavers, concrete sawing, breaking, coring, coloring</td><td>Sawcutting, pavers</td></tr>
<tr><td>C-61/D-12</td><td>Synthetic Products</td><td>Lists synthetic turf and PVC piping for irrigation and drainage</td><td>Synthetic turf</td></tr>
<tr><td>C-61/D-34</td><td>Prefabricated Equipment</td><td>Lists playground equipment and bus stop shelters</td><td>Playground equipment</td></tr>
<tr><td>C-61/D-49</td><td>Tree Service</td><td>Prunes and removes trees, limbs, stumps (including grinding), guying</td><td>Tree removal and pruning</td></tr>
<tr><td>C-61/D-59</td><td>Hydroseed Spraying</td><td>Applies seed through liquid to surfaces prepared by others</td><td>Hydroseed subs</td></tr>
<tr><td>C-61/D-63</td><td>Construction Clean-up</td><td>Removes construction debris from grounds</td><td>Debris haul-off</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>`,
  watch: `<p>When you hire a sub, check their license on CSLB's Instant License Check for the right class, an active status, the bond and workers' comp. A sub with the wrong class makes your bid weaker, and on public work it can make your listed sub a problem.</p>`,
  src: "CSLB classification pages for A, C-7, C-8, C-10, C-12, C-13, C-21, C-29, C-42, C-53 and C-61 subcategories D-6, D-12, D-34, D-49, D-59, D-63: <a href=\"https://www.cslb.ca.gov/About_us/Library/Licensing_Classifications/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">cslb.ca.gov licensing classifications</a> (checked October 2026)."
},

{ h: "Scope responsibility matrix",
  lead: "Who usually does each scope, whether the landscape sub usually carries it, and which license fits. Use it as a starting point. The bid documents always win.",
  body: `<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Scope</th><th>Typically by</th><th>Landscape sub?</th><th>License that fits</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Remove existing plants, irrigation, mulch</td><td>Landscape</td><td>Yes</td><td>C-27</td></tr>
<tr><td>Clearing and grubbing (light vegetation)</td><td>Grading or landscape</td><td>Often</td><td>C-27 (for landscape), C-12, A</td></tr>
<tr><td>Tree and stump removal</td><td>Tree service or landscape</td><td>Sometimes</td><td>D-49; C-27 when part of landscape work</td></tr>
<tr><td>Concrete and asphalt demo, sawcut</td><td>Demo, grading or concrete sub</td><td>Small areas only</td><td>C-12, C-21, D-6, A; C-27 when incidental</td></tr>
<tr><td>Rough grading, cut/fill, import/export</td><td>Grading contractor</td><td>Rarely</td><td>C-12, A</td></tr>
<tr><td>Fine grading of planting areas</td><td>Landscape</td><td>Yes</td><td>C-27</td></tr>
<tr><td>SWPPP writing (QSD), BMP inspections (QSP)</td><td>GC or prime, with consultant</td><td>Prime: yes; sub: no</td><td>QSD/QSP certificates, not a license class</td></tr>
<tr><td>Fiber rolls, hydroseed, erosion blanket</td><td>Landscape or erosion control sub</td><td>Often</td><td>C-27, D-59 (hydroseed)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Area drains in planting areas</td><td>Landscape or plumbing</td><td>Often</td><td>C-27 (landscape drainage)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Storm drain pipe, catch basins, structures</td><td>Civil contractor</td><td>Rarely</td><td>A, C-42, C-34</td></tr>
<tr><td>Bioretention soil media and planting</td><td>Civil for structure, landscape for soil and plants</td><td>Planting yes; basin often no</td><td>A for basin; C-27 for planting</td></tr>
<tr><td>Concrete walks, plazas, curbs</td><td>Concrete sub</td><td>Rarely</td><td>C-8</td></tr>
<tr><td>Mow curb, edging, header board</td><td>Landscape</td><td>Yes</td><td>C-27</td></tr>
<tr><td>Pavers</td><td>Paver or masonry sub</td><td>Sometimes</td><td>D-6, C-29, C-27 in landscape</td></tr>
<tr><td>DG paving, boulders, cobble, stepping stones</td><td>Landscape</td><td>Yes</td><td>C-27</td></tr>
<tr><td>Retaining walls, structural seat walls</td><td>Concrete or masonry sub</td><td>Low garden walls only</td><td>C-8, C-29 (engineered design)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Benches, trash cans, bike racks</td><td>Landscape or concrete sub</td><td>Often</td><td>C-27 when part of landscape; D-34</td></tr>
<tr><td>Playground equipment, safety surfacing</td><td>Playground installer</td><td>Rarely</td><td>D-34, C-13 (game equipment), A</td></tr>
<tr><td>Fencing and gates</td><td>Fence sub</td><td>Rarely</td><td>C-13</td></tr>
<tr><td>Shade structures</td><td>GC or specialty</td><td>No</td><td>B, plus building permit</td></tr>
<tr><td>Controller power, line-voltage lights</td><td>Electrical sub</td><td>No (sub it)</td><td>C-10</td></tr>
<tr><td>Low-voltage landscape lighting</td><td>Lighting or landscape</td><td>Sometimes</td><td>C-7 (listed by name); C-10</td></tr>
<tr><td>Synthetic turf</td><td>Turf installer</td><td>Sometimes</td><td>D-12 (listed by name); C-27</td></tr>
<tr><td>Water meter, service line to POC</td><td>Water agency, plumbing or civil</td><td>Rarely</td><td>Agency; C-36, A</td></tr>
<tr><td>Backflow preventer and enclosure</td><td>Landscape or plumbing</td><td>Often</td><td>C-27 (irrigation), C-36</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sleeves under paving</td><td>Landscape (before paving)</td><td>Usually</td><td>C-27</td></tr>
<tr><td>Plant establishment, maintenance</td><td>Landscape</td><td>Yes</td><td>C-27</td></tr>
<tr><td>Traffic control, mobilization, sweeping</td><td>Prime</td><td>Only when you're prime</td><td>Traffic control sub as needed</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>`,
  watch: `<p>“Typically” varies by GC, by agency and by region. One GC puts the area drains with plumbing, the next one with landscape. Read the scope sheet for every job, and when two packages both seem to include an item, ask.</p>`,
  src: "License classes: CSLB classification pages (cslb.ca.gov). The “typically by” column is general industry practice; confirm on every job."
},

{ h: "Three answers for every extra scope",
  lead: "For each item that isn't irrigation or planting, pick one answer on purpose: do it yourself, sub it out, or exclude it.",
  body: `<ol class="steps">
<li><div><b>Can we legally do it?</b>Check the C-27 text and the incidental-and-supplemental rule. If no, you sub it or exclude it.</div></li>
<li><div><b>Do we have the crew, the equipment and the know-how?</b>A skid steer and a 3-person crew can remove 500 SF of old planter concrete. They can't set a 6-foot engineered wall.</div></li>
<li><div><b>Is it big enough to matter, and small enough to risk?</b>A $2,000 item you price wrong costs little. A $150,000 wall you price wrong can sink the job.</div></li>
<li class="hl"><div><b>If you include it, can you price it with real quotes?</b>No quote, no number. Plugging a guess for someone else's trade is how estimators lose money.</div></li>
<li><div><b>Does the customer want one price?</b>Many GCs prefer one landscape number with the small extras inside. Public primes must cover every item anyway.</div></li>
</ol>
<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Answer</th><th>When to use it</th><th>What to write</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Self-perform</td><td>Licensed, equipped, can price it</td><td>List it as its own line in your scope</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sub it out</td><td>Not your trade, but the customer wants it in your number</td><td>“Includes [item] by a licensed [class] subcontractor”</td></tr>
<tr><td>Exclude</td><td>Not your trade, too risky, or another package covers it</td><td>“Excludes [item]; by [GC/others]”</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>`,
  deeper: `<p>On public jobs you bid as prime, excluding isn't allowed. The bid item list is the contract. You either self-perform each item or list a subcontractor for it (Module 14 covers the listing law). That's why the decision about public jobs starts at go/no-go (Module 3): if a Caltrans landscape job has $400,000 of walls, you need a wall sub before you decide to bid.</p>`
},

{ h: "Demolition and removals · What they are",
  lead: "Demo means taking out what's there so new work can go in. In landscape packages it comes in a few common types, each with its own unit.",
  body: `<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>What it is</th><th>Usual unit</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Remove existing landscape</td><td>Pull plants, groundcover, turf, mulch, edging</td><td>SF or LS</td></tr>
<tr><td>Remove irrigation</td><td>Heads, valves, controllers, pipe (or abandon pipe in place if allowed)</td><td>LS, EA, LF</td></tr>
<tr><td>Clearing and grubbing</td><td>Remove vegetation, roots, stumps and debris so grading can start</td><td>LS, SF or acre</td></tr>
<tr><td>Roadside clearing (Caltrans)</td><td>Mow, remove trash and debris, control weeds along the work</td><td>LS</td></tr>
<tr><td>Tree and stump removal</td><td>Cut, haul, grind stump below grade</td><td>EA by trunk diameter</td></tr>
<tr><td>Concrete and asphalt demo</td><td>Sawcut edges, break, load, haul</td><td>SF, SY or CY; sawcut by LF</td></tr>
<tr><td>Salvage</td><td>Remove carefully and deliver to the owner</td><td>EA or LS</td></tr>
<tr><td>Protect in place</td><td>Leave it and keep it from damage (trees, utilities, paving)</td><td>LS or EA; fence by LF</td></tr>
<tr><td>Haul-off and disposal</td><td>Trucking plus dump or recycling fees</td><td>Loads, tons or CY</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>`,
  ex: `<p>Real Caltrans removal items: “Remove Irrigation Facility” and “Salvage Irrigation Facility” (11-428704 items 34 and 35, both LS); “Remove Concrete (CY)” 66 CY (11-422004 item 16); “Remove Asphalt Concrete Dike” 330 LF (11-428804 item 83); “Remove Chain Link Fence” 10 LF (11-428804 item 88); “Remove Retaining Wall (LS)” (11-4C0034 item 56).</p>`,
  watch: `<p>“Remove” on a Caltrans plan usually includes disposal. “Salvage” means you deliver it somewhere, in usable condition. “Abandon” means leave it in the ground, often capped or filled. Each costs a different amount. Read the note, not just the symbol.</p>`,
  src: "Caltrans Bid Item Lists: 11-428704, 11-422004, 11-428804, 11-4C0034."
},

{ h: "Demolition · How it shows up in real documents",
  lead: "Demo can be its own bid item, or it can be buried inside another item. The second kind is the dangerous one.",
  body: `<div class="twocol">
<div><h3>Paid as its own item</h3><ul>
<li>Caltrans: Clearing and Grubbing (LS), Remove Concrete (CY), Remove Irrigation Facility (LS)</li>
<li>Private: “Demo existing planters” as a line on the GC's bid form</li>
<li>You price it, you get paid for it, and the GC can compare it</li>
</ul></div>
<div><h3>Buried inside another item</h3><ul>
<li>City of San Diego jobs often fold clearing and grubbing into a lump sum</li>
<li>Caltrans: “Dispose of mowed material during roadside clearing”</li>
<li>Specs saying “remove all existing improvements in conflict with the work”</li>
<li>You must find it, take it off, and spread its cost into your price</li>
</ul></div>
</div>`,
  ex: `<p>Real example, City of San Diego K-24-1952 (EMTS Boat Dock Esplanade, bid December 6, 2023, estimate $1,600,000). Section 300-1.1 says clearing and grubbing “shall also include saw cutting, demolition, removal and disposal of all existing improvements called out on the Plans to be removed,” including “excess soil, pavement, concrete pavers, concrete ribbon gutter, concrete mow curb, curb and base course, sidewalk to nearest joint, curb and gutter… signs and sign posts, vegetation.” Section 300-1.4 says it is paid inside the lump sum “Construction of Park Improvements.” No separate payment.</p>`,
  watch: `<p>The same section tells the bidder to “inspect the project site to verify the magnitude and cost of all Clearing and Grubbing.” That sentence puts the risk on you. Walk the site, photograph what's there, and measure it. If you're the landscape sub, ask the prime in writing which removals they expect from you.</p>`,
  src: "City of San Diego K-24-1952-DBB-3-A Supplementary Special Provisions 300-1.1 item 10 and 300-1.4 items 3–4; Notice Inviting Bids items 3 and 6."
},

{ h: "Demolition · Taking it off",
  lead: "Measure what comes out the same way you measure what goes in, then turn it into the units that drive cost: loads, tons and hours.",
  body: `<ol class="steps">
<li><div><b>Mark the demo plan</b>Color every removal by type: concrete, asphalt, planting, irrigation, trees, items to salvage, items to protect.</div></li>
<li><div><b>Measure</b>Concrete and asphalt by SF with the thickness from the plans, a site visit or a core. Sawcut by LF along the removal edge. Trees by count and trunk size. Planting by SF.</div></li>
<li class="hl"><div><b>Convert to in-place CY</b>CY = SF × thickness in inches ÷ 324. This is the “bank” or in-place volume.</div></li>
<li><div><b>Add swell for hauling</b>Broken material takes more room than in place. Loose CY = in-place CY × swell factor. Ask your hauler what they see.</div></li>
<li><div><b>Convert to tons for dump fees</b>Normal concrete weighs about 145–150 lb per cubic foot. 150 × 27 = 4,050 lb, so about 2 tons per in-place CY.</div></li>
<li><div><b>Count truck loads</b>Loads = loose CY ÷ truck capacity, rounded up. Weight limits can cut a load before the bed is full with concrete.</div></li>
</ol>`,
  watch: `<p>Hidden thickness is the classic demo trap. A “4-inch” patio can be 6 inches at the edges, or have rebar or a second slab under it. If the plans don't say, write your assumed thickness in the proposal.</p>`,
  deeper: `<p>Green waste, clean concrete, clean soil and mixed debris usually go to different places at different rates. Concrete and asphalt recyclers often charge much less than a landfill, and some take clean loads cheaply. Call the facilities you will use for current rates before every bid; they change.</p>`
},

{ h: "Worked example · Removing an old concrete patio",
  lead: "A GC asks you to remove a 600 SF concrete patio inside a new planting area. Here's every line of math.",
  exLabel: "Practice example (made-up rates; get real quotes from your hauler, recycler and sawcut sub)",
  ex: `<p><strong>Quantities</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Area 600 SF, 4" thick. In-place CY = 600 × 4 ÷ 324 = 2,400 ÷ 324 = <strong>7.41 CY</strong></li>
<li>Swell example 1.50 for broken concrete: 7.41 × 1.50 = <strong>11.1 loose CY</strong></li>
<li>Weight: 7.41 CY × 2.0 tons = <strong>14.8 tons</strong></li>
<li>Loads at 10 loose CY per truck: 11.1 ÷ 10 = 1.11, round up to <strong>2 loads</strong></li>
<li>Sawcut where the patio meets the walk that stays: <strong>120 LF</strong></li>
</ul>
<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Cost line</th><th>Math</th><th class="num">Cost</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Sawcut sub (example $4.00/LF)</td><td>120 × $4.00</td><td class="num">$480.00</td></tr>
<tr><td>Skid steer with breaker rental (example)</td><td>1 day</td><td class="num">$450.00</td></tr>
<tr><td>Labor: operator + laborer, 1 day</td><td>16 LH × $36.00</td><td class="num">$576.00</td></tr>
<tr><td>Trucking (example $130/hr, 2.5 hr per load)</td><td>2 × 2.5 × $130</td><td class="num">$650.00</td></tr>
<tr><td>Recycler fee (example $60/ton)</td><td>14.8 × $60</td><td class="num">$888.00</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Direct cost</strong></td><td>480 + 450 + 576 + 650 + 888</td><td class="num"><strong>$3,044.00</strong></td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>Per SF: $3,044 ÷ 600 = <strong>$5.07/SF</strong>. Per in-place CY: $3,044 ÷ 7.41 = <strong>$410.80/CY</strong>. Then add overhead and markup (Module 10).</p>`,
  body: `<p>The $36.00 labor hour is the average from the Module 9 practice crew. Compare with real Caltrans bids: “Remove Concrete (CY),” 66 CY on 11-422004, drew $200, $200, $240 and $342 per CY from four bidders. Your small job costs more per CY because the sawcut, the rental day and the truck minimums don't shrink with the quantity.</p>`,
  watch: `<p>Small demo jobs are mostly minimums: a day of rental, a half day of trucking, a sawcut sub's trip charge. Never price small demo by multiplying a big-job unit price by a small quantity.</p>`,
  src: "Caltrans 11-422004 Bid Summary, item 16 (Remove Concrete (CY)), unit prices of all four bidders."
},

{ h: "Trees, salvage and protecting what stays",
  lead: "Some of the most expensive mistakes in demo aren't removals. They're damage to things that were supposed to stay.",
  body: `<div class="twocol">
<div><h3>Tree and stump removal</h3><ul>
<li>Count by trunk size (for example 6–12", 12–24", over 24")</li>
<li>Price cutting, chipping or hauling, and stump grinding depth</li>
<li>Big or risky trees: get a tree service quote (D-49 lists tree removal, stump grinding and guying)</li>
<li>Check for permits, nesting-bird rules and protected trees on public jobs</li>
</ul></div>
<div><h3>Protect in place</h3><ul>
<li>Tree protection fence at the drip line or the zone shown</li>
<li>No trenching, parking or stockpiles in the zone</li>
<li>Hand dig or bore near roots if the plans say so</li>
<li>Price the fence, its upkeep, and the slower work near it</li>
</ul></div>
</div>`,
  ex: `<p>Real example, 11-428704: item 19 “Temporary Protection Fence (Rope),” 1,120 LF. The special provisions require posts at most 10 feet apart, embedded at least 24 inches, with yellow 3/8" polypropylene rope tied with a clove hitch, and replacement of anything damaged. Bids ranged from $0.37 to $10.00 per LF. The same job's salvage item required hauling salvaged irrigation material to the Caltrans Kearny Mesa Landscape Office at 7181 Opportunity Road, with 2 business days' notice. Bids for “Salvage Irrigation Facility” ranged from $500 to $10,000.</p>`,
  watch: `<p>Specs often make you replace a damaged tree that was supposed to be protected, sometimes with a large box size or a value set by an arborist. One damaged mature tree can cost more than the whole protection item. Read the tree protection section before you price.</p>`,
  src: "11-428704 Special Provisions 16-2.06 (Temporary Protection Fence (Rope)) and 15-1.03C (salvage stockpile); 11-428704 Bid Summary items 19 and 35; CSLB D-49 Tree Service classification."
},

{ h: "Demolition · Risks and exclusion language",
  lead: "Demo hides surprises. Price what you can see, and write down what you assumed.",
  body: `<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Risk</th><th>What it costs</th><th>How to handle it</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Thicker concrete, rebar, buried slabs</td><td>More breaking time, more tons</td><td>State assumed thickness; unit price for extra</td></tr>
<tr><td>Buried debris and rock in planting areas</td><td>Hand work, more haul</td><td>Exclude removal of buried debris from planting areas, or give a unit price</td></tr>
<tr><td>Hazardous materials (lead, asbestos, contaminated soil)</td><td>Special handling and disposal</td><td>Always exclude; Caltrans jobs list Aerially Deposited Lead items separately</td></tr>
<tr><td>Utilities found during demo</td><td>Stops work, repairs</td><td>DigAlert, potholing, clarify who repairs unmarked lines</td></tr>
<tr><td>Dump fees rise between bid and work</td><td>Margin loss</td><td>Price-validity date; check rates before bid</td></tr>
<tr><td>Damage to items to remain</td><td>Repair or replacement</td><td>Photos before work; protection priced in</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p><strong>Proposal wording (adjust to the job):</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>“Includes removal and disposal of existing planting, irrigation and mulch within the planting areas shown on L-1.”</li>
<li>“Includes removal of the existing 4-inch concrete patio (approx. 600 SF) with sawcut at the walk to remain. Thicker concrete, reinforcing or buried slabs at $___ per SF additional.”</li>
<li>“Excludes demolition of paving, curbs, walls, structures and utilities not listed above.”</li>
<li>“Excludes removal of buried debris, rock over 6 inches and contaminated or hazardous materials.”</li>
</ul>`,
  ex: `<p>Real example: 11-428804 and 11-430674 each had a separate item for “Roadway Excavation (Type Z-2) (Aerially Deposited Lead).” On 11-430674, bidders priced it at $229.80 to $422.00 per CY, while ordinary Roadway Excavation on the same job went for $22.00 to $60.30 per CY. Contaminated soil can cost ten times more to move.</p>`,
  src: "Caltrans 11-430674 Bid Summary items 26 and 27; 11-428804 items 27 and 28."
},

{ h: "Earthwork · Rough grading vs. fine grading",
  lead: "Rough grading shapes the whole site, usually by the grading contractor. Fine grading is the last few inches in planting areas, usually by you. The hand-off between them is where money is lost.",
  body: `<div class="twocol">
<div><h3>Rough (mass) grading</h3><ul>
<li>Cuts and fills the site to the grading plan</li>
<li>Big equipment: dozers, scrapers, excavators, rollers</li>
<li>Compaction tested by the soils engineer</li>
<li>Done by a C-12 or A contractor, or the GC's grading sub</li>
<li>Leaves planting areas “at rough grade” within a tolerance</li>
</ul></div>
<div><h3>Fine (finish) grading</h3><ul>
<li>Shapes planting areas to finish grade, smooth and draining</li>
<li>Skid steer, small tractor, rakes</li>
<li>Rounds tops and bottoms of slopes, removes clods and rock</li>
<li>Done by the landscape contractor (C-27 covers grading <em>for</em> landscape)</li>
<li>Includes setting soil below walks for mulch</li>
</ul></div>
</div>
<ol class="steps">
<li class="hl"><div><b>The hand-off number</b>Your proposal should say what rough grade you expect to receive. A common clarification: “Rough grade delivered to within ±0.1 ft of finish grade, less the depth of soil amendments and mulch, by others.” 0.1 ft = 1.2 inches.</div></li>
<li><div><b>Walk it before you start</b>Check the grades with a level or laser before you accept the area. Write down where it's high or low, and send it to the GC.</div></li>
</ol>`,
  watch: `<p>Rough grade delivered “to finish grade” in a shrub bed that needs 3 inches of mulch is 3 inches high everywhere. On a big site that's hundreds of cubic yards of soil you didn't price to export. See the worked example two slides ahead.</p>`
},

{ h: "Who sets the grades, and how you read them",
  lead: "The civil engineer designs the grades. A surveyor stakes them. The landscape plans and specs tell you what finish grade means for planting.",
  body: `<ul>
<li><strong>Grading plan (civil sheets):</strong> spot elevations, contours, slopes in percent, pad and walk elevations. Finish grade is often marked FG, finish surface FS, top of curb TC, flow line FL.</li>
<li><strong>Surveyor's staking:</strong> stakes or marks with cut (C) or fill (F) numbers. On private jobs, the GC usually pays for survey. If your bid form says “staking by landscape,” price a surveyor.</li>
<li><strong>Landscape details:</strong> how far finish soil sits below walks, curbs and mow strips in turf, shrub and DG areas.</li>
<li><strong>Specs:</strong> surface slopes, smoothness and who accepts the grades.</li>
</ul>`,
  ex: `<p>Real example, City of San Diego K-24-1952, section 801-2.3 Finish Grading: “Planting surfaces shall be graded with no less than one percent (1%) surface slope for positive drainage,” grades rounded at all changes in slope, undulations “leveled and floated out before planting,” and “Final grades shall be acceptable to the Resident Engineer before planting operations will be allowed to begin.” That last line means an inspection and a possible wait before planting.</p>
<p>Common detail notes say something like “finish grade 1 inch below walk in turf areas” and “finish soil grade 3 inches below walk in mulched areas.” These are examples. Use the numbers on your job's details.</p>`,
  watch: `<p>Never set grades by eye on a commercial job. If the plans show no elevations in planting areas and nobody is staking, send an RFI (Module 12) asking who provides grades. Ponding water next to a building is a callback you pay for.</p>`,
  src: "City of San Diego K-24-1952-DBB-3-A, 801-2.3 Finish Grading, items 5–9."
},

{ h: "Cubic yard math: bank, loose and compacted",
  lead: "Soil changes size when you dig it and when you compact it. Haul, buy and place it in the right kind of cubic yard.",
  body: `<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Used for</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Bank CY (BCY)</td><td>Soil in place, before digging</td><td>Cut quantities on grading plans</td></tr>
<tr><td>Loose CY (LCY)</td><td>Soil after digging, fluffed up</td><td>Truck loads, haul and dump fees</td></tr>
<tr><td>Compacted CY (CCY)</td><td>Soil placed and compacted</td><td>Fill quantities on grading plans</td></tr>
<tr><td>Swell factor</td><td>LCY ÷ BCY (above 1.00)</td><td>Bank to loose</td></tr>
<tr><td>Shrink factor</td><td>CCY ÷ BCY (usually below 1.00)</td><td>Bank to compacted</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<ol class="steps">
<li><div><b>Cut to haul</b>LCY = BCY × swell factor</div></li>
<li><div><b>Fill needed from bank</b>BCY needed = CCY ÷ shrink factor</div></li>
<li class="hl"><div><b>Import trucks</b>Loads = (BCY needed × swell factor) ÷ CY per truck, round up</div></li>
</ol>`,
  ex: `<p><strong>Worked example (example factors: swell 1.25, shrink 0.90; get real factors from the soils report or your grading sub):</strong> you need 200 CCY of fill.</p>
<ul>
<li>BCY needed = 200 ÷ 0.90 = <strong>222.2 BCY</strong></li>
<li>LCY to truck in = 222.2 × 1.25 = <strong>277.8 LCY</strong></li>
<li>Loads at 10 LCY each = 27.8, round up to <strong>28 loads</strong></li>
</ul>
<p>If you had just ordered 200 CY, you'd be about 78 loose CY (8 loads) short.</p>`,
  watch: `<p>Swell and shrink depend on the soil. Sand, clay and rock behave very differently. Treat any textbook factor as a placeholder and ask the soils report, the grading sub or the supplier.</p>`
},

{ h: "Worked example · When the rough grade comes in high",
  lead: "An 8,000 SF shrub area needs finish soil 3 inches below the walk for mulch. The grading sub left it right at walk height. Somebody has to export the extra soil.",
  exLabel: "Practice example (made-up rates; use your own trucking, dump and equipment costs)",
  ex: `<p><strong>Quantities</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Extra soil: 8,000 SF × 3" ÷ 324 = 24,000 ÷ 324 = <strong>74.1 BCY</strong></li>
<li>Loose (example swell 1.25): 74.1 × 1.25 = <strong>92.6 LCY</strong>, call it 93</li>
<li>Loads at 10 LCY: 93 ÷ 10 = 9.3, round up to <strong>10 loads</strong></li>
</ul>
<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Cost line</th><th>Math</th><th class="num">Cost</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Trucking (example $130/hr, 2 hr per load)</td><td>10 × 2 × $130</td><td class="num">$2,600.00</td></tr>
<tr><td>Clean soil disposal (example $100/load)</td><td>10 × $100</td><td class="num">$1,000.00</td></tr>
<tr><td>Skid steer rental (example $350/day)</td><td>2 days × $350</td><td class="num">$700.00</td></tr>
<tr><td>Operator + laborer</td><td>2 days × 16 LH × $36.00 = 32 LH</td><td class="num">$1,152.00</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Direct cost</strong></td><td>2,600 + 1,000 + 700 + 1,152</td><td class="num"><strong>$5,452.00</strong></td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>That's $5,452 ÷ 8,000 = <strong>$0.68 per SF</strong> of cost that was never in the landscape price.</p>`,
  body: `<p>If your proposal said “rough grade delivered to ±0.1 ft of finish grade less mulch and amendment depth, by others,” this is a change order to the GC. If your proposal said nothing, the GC will say fine grading was yours. One sentence decides who pays $5,452.</p>`,
  watch: `<p>Amendments add volume too. Tilling 6 CY of compost per 1,000 SF into the soil raises the grade. Count it when you figure how much soil must come out.</p>`
},

{ h: "Import, export, compaction and testing",
  lead: "Soil that comes in or goes out costs trucking, fees and paperwork. Soil that gets compacted costs tests.",
  body: `<div class="twocol">
<div><h3>Import (bringing soil in)</h3><ul>
<li>Spec often requires tested topsoil or a soil approved by sample</li>
<li>Price: material per CY or ton, trucking, spreading, testing</li>
<li>Ask for the supplier's test report before you buy</li>
</ul></div>
<div><h3>Export (hauling soil out)</h3><ul>
<li>Clean soil may go to another job or a fill site; mixed or contaminated soil costs much more</li>
<li>Some sites ask for soil tests before they accept a load</li>
<li>Price: loading, trucking, dump fee per load or ton</li>
</ul></div>
</div>
<p><strong>Compaction.</strong> Specs give compaction as a percent of the maximum density from a lab test (relative compaction). Planting soil is usually left loose for roots. Paving bases, wall backfill, boulder bases and DG subgrade are compacted.</p>`,
  ex: `<p>Real spec examples: Caltrans rock mulch (11-422004, 20-5.03E) “compact to not less than 90 percent relative compaction.” Caltrans decorative boulders (20-5.06C): “Compact subgrade under the boulder to not less than 90 percent relative compaction.” City of San Diego stabilized DG (K-24-1952, 200-5.3.3): compact subgrade to at least 90%, measured per ASTM D1557, and 200-5.3.4 requires written compaction tests “by a certified testing laboratory” for each lift, as “the sole financial responsibility of the Contractor.”</p>`,
  watch: `<p>Who pays for testing changes by job. On many private jobs the owner's testing lab is paid by the owner, but retests after a failed test are on you. On the City DG spec above, all testing is on the contractor. Price the tests and the time waiting for results.</p>`,
  src: "11-422004 Special Provisions 20-5.03E(3) and 20-5.06C; City of San Diego K-24-1952 200-5.1.1, 200-5.3.2, 200-5.3.3, 200-5.3.4."
},

{ h: "Soil prep, ripping and “incorporate materials”",
  lead: "Ripping and tilling are landscape work, but compacted subgrade from mass grading can turn a one-pass job into a three-pass job.",
  body: `<ul>
<li><strong>Ripping:</strong> breaking compacted soil with ripper shanks behind a tractor or dozer before tilling. Needed where grading compaction or construction traffic packed the planting areas.</li>
<li><strong>Tilling / incorporating:</strong> mixing amendments into the top 6 inches (or the depth the spec says). Module 7 covers the amendment takeoff.</li>
<li><strong>Cross-ripping:</strong> ripping in two directions. Some specs require it; it doubles the passes.</li>
<li><strong>Rock and debris removal:</strong> picking or screening rock over a set size (for example 1 inch) from the top layer.</li>
</ul>`,
  ex: `<p>Real example, 11-4C0034 (I-5/805 San Ysidro gateway, bid May 11, 2023): item 52 “Incorporate Materials,” 73,800 SQFT. The low bidder, Hazard Construction, priced it at $0.20 per SQFT ($14,760). Item 51 “Compost (CY),” 120 CY, was priced at $70.00 per CY. The compost and the work to mix it in were two separate items.</p>`,
  watch: `<p>On sites that were mass graded, ask the GC whether planting areas will be left at the compaction of the building pads. If yes, price ripping. If the specs require soil tests after prep, price the wait and any retreatment.</p>`,
  src: "Caltrans 11-4C0034 Bid Summary (Contract Proposal of Low Bidder), items 51 and 52."
},

{ h: "Earthwork · Licensing, real prices and exclusions",
  lead: "Grading for landscape is yours. Grading a site is a grading contractor's. Price the first and exclude or sub the second.",
  body: `<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Real Caltrans item</th><th>Job</th><th>Quantity</th><th class="num">Range of bids per CY</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Roadway Excavation</td><td>11-422004</td><td>340 CY</td><td class="num">$75.00 – $165.00</td></tr>
<tr><td>Roadway Excavation</td><td>11-428804</td><td>3,620 CY</td><td class="num">$20.00 – $90.10</td></tr>
<tr><td>Roadway Excavation</td><td>11-430674</td><td>6,480 CY</td><td class="num">$22.00 – $60.30</td></tr>
<tr><td>Roadway Excavation</td><td>11-4C0034 (low bid)</td><td>180 CY</td><td class="num">$125.00</td></tr>
<tr><td>Structure Excavation / Backfill</td><td>11-4C0034 (low bid)</td><td>156 / 61 CY</td><td class="num">$35.00 / $90.00</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>Notice the pattern: small quantities cost far more per CY. On 11-422004, a landscape company won with Roadway Excavation at $93.30 per CY; on the same job, the second bidder (a general engineering firm) listed an excavation subcontractor for 93% of that item.</p>
<p><strong>Proposal wording (adjust to the job):</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>“Includes fine grading of planting areas to finish grade per L-series details.”</li>
<li>“Excludes rough grading, cut and fill, import and export of soil, and compaction testing. Rough grade delivered to ±0.1 ft of finish grade, less depth of amendments and mulch, by others.”</li>
<li>“Excludes construction staking. Grades and elevations by others.”</li>
<li>“Import of planting soil, if required, at $___ per CY placed.”</li>
</ul>`,
  deeper: `<p>The A classification lists “land leveling and earthmoving projects, excavating, grading, trenching, paving and surfacing work.” The C-12 covers digging, moving and placing earth for “a cut, fill, excavation, grade, trench, backfill.” The C-27 covers preparing and grading land “for the installation of any architectural, horticultural and decorative treatment.” Grading that serves the landscape fits the C-27; grading a building pad or a street doesn't.</p>`,
  src: "Caltrans Bid Summaries: 11-422004 item 17 and subcontractor list; 11-428804 item 27; 11-430674 item 26; 11-4C0034 items 15–17. CSLB A, C-12 and C-27 classification pages."
},

{ h: "Erosion and sediment control · SWPPP, WPCP and who does what",
  lead: "Storm water rules keep dirt and pollutants out of storm drains. Plans are written by certified people; BMPs are installed and kept up by crews. Know which part is being handed to you.",
  body: `<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Plain meaning</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>CGP</td><td>Construction General Permit, the State Water Board's statewide storm water permit for construction (Order 2022-0057-DWQ, in effect since September 1, 2023)</td></tr>
<tr><td>When the CGP applies</td><td>Projects that disturb 1 acre or more of soil, or less than 1 acre if part of a larger common plan of development of 1 acre or more</td></tr>
<tr><td>SWPPP</td><td>Storm Water Pollution Prevention Plan: the written plan for a CGP project</td></tr>
<tr><td>QSD</td><td>Qualified SWPPP Developer: the certified person who writes and revises the SWPPP</td></tr>
<tr><td>QSP</td><td>Qualified SWPPP Practitioner: the certified person who inspects and runs the BMPs in the field</td></tr>
<tr><td>WPCP</td><td>Water Pollution Control Program (Caltrans) or Plan (City): a shorter plan for jobs under the CGP threshold</td></tr>
<tr><td>BMP</td><td>Best Management Practice: each control, like a fiber roll or inlet protection</td></tr>
<tr><td>Risk level</td><td>1, 2 or 3 under the CGP; higher risk means more inspections, sampling and reports</td></tr>
<tr><td>REAP</td><td>Rain Event Action Plan, prepared before forecast rain on higher-risk sites</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>`,
  ex: `<p>Real examples: 11-428704 says “This project's risk level is 2,” and the special provisions say “You must assign a QSD to develop and revise the SWPPP” and that the WPC manager must meet the CGP requirements for a QSP and hold an 8-hour Caltrans WPC manager training certificate. The City of San Diego job K-24-1952 is “subject to SWPPP Risk Level 1,” and BMP maintenance must be documented “by the QSP or QSD.”</p>`,
  watch: `<p>As a sub to a GC, the SWPPP is normally the GC's. Your exposure is installing BMPs, repairing BMPs your crew damages, and keeping your own stockpiles covered. Say exactly that in your proposal. As a Caltrans prime, the whole SWPPP is yours, so line up a QSD/QSP consultant before bid day.</p>`,
  src: "State Water Resources Control Board, Construction Stormwater Program: <a href=\"https://www.waterboards.ca.gov/water_issues/programs/stormwater/construction.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">waterboards.ca.gov</a> (checked October 2026); 11-428704 Special Provisions 13-3.01A, 13-1.01D(4)(b), 13-3.01C(2)(a); City K-24-1952 1001-1 and 1001-2.10."
},

{ h: "BMPs you may install, and what they really cost",
  lead: "BMPs are taken off by LF, EA or SF from the erosion control plan. Real Caltrans bids show how far prices spread.",
  body: `<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>BMP</th><th>What it is</th><th>Unit</th><th>Real bids (job, item)</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Temporary fiber roll</td><td>Straw or fiber tube staked along contours or edges</td><td>LF</td><td>$1.50–$7.25 (11-431064 #17, 23,800 LF)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Temporary silt fence</td><td>Fabric fence trenched in at the toe of slopes</td><td>LF</td><td>$2.30–$9.00 (11-422004 #11, 1,840 LF)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Temporary drainage inlet protection</td><td>Gravel bags or filter inserts at drain inlets</td><td>EA</td><td>$150–$700 (11-428804 #18, 43 EA)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Temporary construction entrance</td><td>Rock pad or grate to knock mud off tires</td><td>EA</td><td>$2,000–$7,546 (11-430674 #21, 9 EA)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Temporary check dam</td><td>Gravel bags across a ditch to slow water</td><td>LF</td><td>$3.80–$13.34 (11-430674 #19, 760 LF)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Temporary concrete washout</td><td>Lined pit or bin for concrete rinse water</td><td>LS</td><td>$1,000–$20,359 (11-430674 #23)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Hydroseed</td><td>Seed, fiber and tackifier sprayed on</td><td>SQFT</td><td>$0.10–$0.11 (11-431064 #63, 797,000 SQFT)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Bonded fiber matrix</td><td>Sprayed erosion cover that bonds to soil</td><td>SQFT</td><td>$0.25–$1.15 (11-422004 #40, 47,600 SQFT)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Rolled erosion control product (jute mesh)</td><td>Blanket stapled to slopes</td><td>SQFT</td><td>$0.30–$2.00 (11-428804 #79, 45,500 SQFT)</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>`,
  ex: `<p>Some items are set by Caltrans, not by bidders. On 11-428704, 11-428804, 11-430674 and 11-431064, every bidder priced “Rain Event Action Plan” at $500 each and “Storm Water Annual Report” at $2,000 each. When every bidder shows the same price, the price was fixed in the bid documents. Read the special provisions to find these before you spend time pricing them.</p>`,
  watch: `<p>BMPs must be maintained until the area is stabilized, often for months. The install is the easy part. Price inspections after rain, replacing crushed fiber rolls, cleaning sediment from inlet protection and removing everything at the end.</p>`,
  src: "Caltrans Bid Summaries, item numbers as listed (all bidders' unit prices)."
},

{ h: "Worked example · Pricing a small BMP package",
  lead: "A GC asks the landscape sub to install and maintain the erosion controls on a 2.5-acre site for 6 months.",
  exLabel: "Practice example (made-up material prices and production rates; use your supplier quotes and your own rates)",
  ex: `<p><strong>Takeoff from the erosion control plan:</strong> 1,200 LF fiber roll (25-ft rolls), 6 inlet protections, maintenance for 6 months.</p>
<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Cost line</th><th>Math</th><th class="num">Cost</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Fiber rolls (example $40 per 25-ft roll)</td><td>1,200 ÷ 25 = 48 rolls × $40</td><td class="num">$1,920.00</td></tr>
<tr><td>Stakes (detail example: 1 every 4 ft, $1.00 each)</td><td>1,200 ÷ 4 = 300 × $1.00</td><td class="num">$300.00</td></tr>
<tr><td>Install labor (example 0.02 LH/LF)</td><td>1,200 × 0.02 = 24 LH × $36.00</td><td class="num">$864.00</td></tr>
<tr><td>Inlet protection (example $60 material + 1 LH each)</td><td>6 × ($60 + $36)</td><td class="num">$576.00</td></tr>
<tr><td>Maintenance visits after rain (example 8 visits × 2 LH)</td><td>16 LH × $36.00</td><td class="num">$576.00</td></tr>
<tr><td>Replacement material (example 20% of rolls)</td><td>$1,920 × 0.20</td><td class="num">$384.00</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Direct cost</strong></td><td>1,920 + 300 + 864 + 576 + 576 + 384</td><td class="num"><strong>$4,620.00</strong></td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>Fiber roll share: (1,920 + 300 + 864 + 384) ÷ 1,200 LF = $3,468 ÷ 1,200 = <strong>$2.89 per LF</strong> before markup. That sits inside the real Caltrans range of $1.50–$7.25.</p>`,
  body: `<p>Notice that the inspections and maintenance are 21% of the cost ($576 + $384 = $960 of $4,620). Leave them out and your “cheap” BMP price loses money every time it rains.</p>`,
  watch: `<p>Don't agree to “all SWPPP compliance” as a sub. If the scope sheet says it, write: “Includes installation and maintenance of the BMPs shown on sheet EC-1 for [6] months. Excludes SWPPP preparation, QSD/QSP services, sampling, reporting, SMARTS filings and fines.”</p>`
},

{ h: "Dust control and street sweeping",
  lead: "Dust and mud on the street are regulated in San Diego County. Somebody pays for water trucks, track-out control and sweeping.",
  body: `<ul>
<li><strong>San Diego County APCD Rule 55 (Fugitive Dust Control)</strong> applies to commercial construction and demolition.</li>
<li>No visible dust beyond the property line for more than 3 minutes total in any 60-minute period.</li>
<li>Track-out (mud and dirt carried onto paved roads) must be minimized with measures like track-out grates or gravel beds, wheel washing, tarping loads, and removed at the end of each work day.</li>
<li>If a street sweeper removes track-out, it must be a PM10-efficient sweeper certified to South Coast AQMD Rule 1186. Leaf blowers can't be used to remove track-out.</li>
</ul>`,
  ex: `<p>Real Caltrans prices for “Street Sweeping” (LS): 11-422004, $2,450 to $16,500; 11-428704, $2,840 to $25,000; 11-430674, $3,000 to $60,000. Same item, same kind of job, prices 7 to 20 times apart. Some bidders sweep with their own equipment; others carry a sweeper sub for the whole job.</p>`,
  watch: `<p>Dust control water is often forgotten. Who pays for the water truck or the hydrant meter? On a private job, say “Dust control and street sweeping by GC except during our own soil work” or price it. Module 13 lists more general-condition exclusions.</p>`,
  src: "San Diego County APCD Rule 55 (adopted June 24, 2009), sections (a) and (d): <a href=\"https://www.sdapcd.org/content/dam/sdapcd/documents/rules/current-rules/Rule-55.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">sdapcd.org</a>; Caltrans Bid Summaries 11-422004 #12, 11-428704 #17, 11-430674 #22."
},

{ h: "Drainage and stormwater · The parts",
  lead: "Landscape plans show drainage that moves water out of planting areas. Civil plans show drainage that moves water off the site. Know the difference before you price.",
  body: `<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>What it is</th><th>Unit</th><th>Usually by</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Area drain</td><td>Small grated inlet in a planter or lawn, piped away</td><td>EA + LF pipe</td><td>Landscape or plumbing</td></tr>
<tr><td>Catch basin</td><td>Larger inlet box, often concrete, with a sump</td><td>EA</td><td>Civil</td></tr>
<tr><td>French drain / subdrain</td><td>Gravel trench with perforated pipe, often wrapped in fabric</td><td>LF</td><td>Landscape or civil</td></tr>
<tr><td>Wall drain</td><td>Perforated pipe and gravel behind a retaining wall</td><td>LF</td><td>Wall contractor</td></tr>
<tr><td>Downspout connection</td><td>Pipe from roof leaders to a drain line or splash pad</td><td>EA</td><td>Plumbing or landscape</td></tr>
<tr><td>Curb outlet / parkway drain</td><td>Pipe or channel through a curb to the street</td><td>EA</td><td>Civil or concrete</td></tr>
<tr><td>Storm drain main</td><td>Large pipe to the public system</td><td>LF</td><td>Civil (A, C-42)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Bioretention / biofiltration basin</td><td>Planted basin that holds and filters runoff</td><td>SF, CY of media</td><td>Civil builds, landscape plants</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>`,
  ex: `<p>Real example: 11-422004 (I-5 Carmel Valley, a storm water mitigation job) had item 51 “6" Perforated Plastic Pipe Underdrain,” 230 LF, plus Permeable Aggregate Base (35 CY) and Pervious Concrete Pavement (10 CY). Underdrain bids were $12.80, $25.00, $25.00 and $28.00 per LF. The landscape company that won bid the lowest.</p>`,
  watch: `<p>Drain pipe that serves a planter is landscape drainage. Pipe that connects to the public storm drain, crosses a street, or is part of the site's engineered storm water system is civil work, needs inspection, and may need an A or C-42 license. If the landscape plan says “connect to storm drain system by others,” keep it that way in your proposal.</p>`,
  src: "Caltrans 11-422004 Bid Item List and Bid Summary, items 45, 50, 51."
},

{ h: "Bioretention, biofiltration and LID",
  lead: "New San Diego projects treat storm water on site. The planted basins are part civil, part landscape, and the specs are strict.",
  body: `<ul>
<li><strong>LID (Low Impact Development):</strong> designing a site so rain soaks in or gets filtered near where it falls, instead of piping it all away.</li>
<li><strong>Bioretention / biofiltration basin:</strong> a shallow planted basin. Layers from top down are usually mulch or none, engineered soil media, a choking or filter layer, gravel, and often an underdrain pipe and an overflow inlet. Liners are used when water must not soak into the ground.</li>
<li><strong>Engineered soil media (often called biofiltration soil media):</strong> a specified blend of sand, compost and soil with a required infiltration rate. Suppliers make it to order with test reports.</li>
<li><strong>Plants:</strong> chosen to handle both flooding and drought. Specs may forbid fertilizer or certain mulch that floats.</li>
</ul>`,
  deeper: `<p>The City of San Diego's Storm Water Standards (Part 1, BMP Design Manual) require projects to retain the design storm on site where feasible, and to use biofiltration that meets its biofiltration standards when retention isn't feasible. The manual sets the design; your job is to build exactly what the detail and specs show. Check the current edition on <a href=\"https://www.sandiego.gov/sites/default/files/sws_part_1_nov_2017_edition.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">sandiego.gov</a> (November 2017 edition shown; check for newer).</p>`,
  watch: `<p>Common money-losers: media that fails the infiltration test and must be dug out, sediment from the site washing into a finished basin before the site is stabilized, and grading the basin bottom out of tolerance. Write in your proposal: “Basin excavation, liner, underdrain, gravel layers and overflow structures by others. Includes furnishing and placing [engineered soil media] and planting per L-series. Protection of completed basins from construction runoff by GC.”</p>`,
  src: "City of San Diego Storm Water Standards, Part 1: BMP Design Manual (November 2017 edition), sandiego.gov."
},

{ h: "Worked example · A planter area drain system",
  lead: "Six area drains in planters, piped to a curb outlet that the civil contractor builds.",
  exLabel: "Practice example (made-up prices and rates; get supplier quotes and use your own production)",
  ex: `<p><strong>Takeoff:</strong> 6 area drains (EA), 180 LF of 4" solid drain pipe, 2 cleanouts. Connection at the curb outlet stub is by others.</p>
<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Cost line</th><th>Math</th><th class="num">Cost</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Area drain inlets with grates (example $45)</td><td>6 × $45</td><td class="num">$270.00</td></tr>
<tr><td>4" pipe (example $2.50/LF)</td><td>180 × $2.50</td><td class="num">$450.00</td></tr>
<tr><td>Fittings allowance (example 10% of pipe)</td><td>$450 × 0.10</td><td class="num">$45.00</td></tr>
<tr><td>Cleanouts (example $35)</td><td>2 × $35</td><td class="num">$70.00</td></tr>
<tr><td>Trench, lay, backfill (example 0.08 LH/LF)</td><td>180 × 0.08 = 14.4 LH</td><td></td></tr>
<tr><td>Set drains (1.0 LH each), cleanouts (0.5 LH each), flush test (2 LH)</td><td>6 + 1 + 2 = 9 LH</td><td></td></tr>
<tr><td>Labor total</td><td>23.4 LH × $36.00</td><td class="num">$842.40</td></tr>
<tr><td>Trencher rental (example)</td><td>1 day</td><td class="num">$250.00</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Direct cost (before tax)</strong></td><td>270 + 450 + 45 + 70 + 842.40 + 250</td><td class="num"><strong>$1,927.40</strong></td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>Per drain, all in: $1,927.40 ÷ 6 = <strong>$321.23</strong>. Add sales tax on materials at the job's rate (Module 8), then overhead and markup.</p>`,
  body: `<p>Check the fall. Drain pipe needs slope, often at least 1% (1.2 inches per 100 feet; check the detail). Over 180 LF that's at least 1.8 inches of drop. If the planters are flat and the curb outlet is high, the pipe can't drain. Ask before you price, not after you dig.</p>`
},

{ h: "Hardscape · Who does what",
  lead: "Some hardscape belongs to landscape, most belongs to concrete and masonry. The line is set by the scope sheet, the license and your crew.",
  body: `<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Hardscape item</th><th>Usually</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Concrete walks, plazas, driveways</td><td>Concrete sub (C-8)</td><td>Exclude unless you sub it</td></tr>
<tr><td>Concrete mow curb, band, edge</td><td>Landscape</td><td>Small pours, often extruded</td></tr>
<tr><td>Steel or aluminum edging, header board</td><td>Landscape</td><td>LF, per detail</td></tr>
<tr><td>Decomposed granite (plain or stabilized)</td><td>Landscape</td><td>SF and CY; testing on stabilized DG</td></tr>
<tr><td>Interlocking pavers</td><td>Paver sub (D-6) or landscape</td><td>Base, sand, edge restraint</td></tr>
<tr><td>Stone or block seat walls</td><td>Masonry (C-29) or concrete (C-8)</td><td>Footings, rebar, caps</td></tr>
<tr><td>Retaining walls</td><td>Concrete or masonry sub with engineered design</td><td>Exclude or sub</td></tr>
<tr><td>Boulders, cobble, stepping stones</td><td>Landscape</td><td>EA and SF</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>`,
  watch: `<p>Module 7 shows how to take off DG, rock, edging and boulders. This module focuses on deciding, pricing and writing them. If the scope sheet lumps “all site concrete” into landscape, call the GC. Most landscape subs either sub it to a concrete contractor at a markup or exclude it.</p>`,
  deeper: `<p>The C-8 classification covers forming, pouring, placing and finishing “mass, pavement, flat and other concrete work.” The D-6 subcategory lists installing “interlocking precast pavers” and concrete sawing and breaking. The C-29 covers block, brick and stone walls and fences “with or without mortar.” Low garden walls and edging as part of landscape fit the C-27 description of landscape facilities; structural walls are a different matter (next slides).</p>`,
  src: "CSLB C-8, C-29 and C-61/D-6 classification pages (cslb.ca.gov)."
},

{ h: "Worked example · Concrete mow curb",
  lead: "400 LF of 6-inch by 6-inch concrete mow curb between turf and shrub areas.",
  exLabel: "Practice example (made-up prices and rates; get a ready-mix quote and use your own production)",
  ex: `<ul>
<li>Cross-section: 0.5 ft × 0.5 ft = 0.25 SF</li>
<li>Volume: 400 LF × 0.25 SF = 100 CF ÷ 27 = <strong>3.70 CY</strong></li>
<li>With 10% waste: 3.70 × 1.10 = <strong>4.07 CY</strong>; order 4.5 CY</li>
<li>Rebar (detail example: one #3 bar continuous, 20-ft bars, lap allowance): 400 ÷ 20 = 20 bars + 2 for laps = <strong>22 bars</strong></li>
</ul>
<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Cost line</th><th>Math</th><th class="num">Cost</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Ready-mix (example $200/CY delivered)</td><td>4.5 × $200</td><td class="num">$900.00</td></tr>
<tr><td>Short-load fee (example)</td><td>1 load under the plant minimum</td><td class="num">$150.00</td></tr>
<tr><td>Rebar (example $8.00 per bar)</td><td>22 × $8.00</td><td class="num">$176.00</td></tr>
<tr><td>Labor: form, place, finish, joints (example 0.06 LH/LF)</td><td>400 × 0.06 = 24 LH × $36.00</td><td class="num">$864.00</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Direct cost</strong></td><td>900 + 150 + 176 + 864</td><td class="num"><strong>$2,090.00</strong></td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>Per LF: $2,090 ÷ 400 = <strong>$5.23/LF</strong> before overhead and markup.</p>`,
  body: `<p>Compare a real landscape edging price: Caltrans “Edging” (LF) on 11-431064, 6,200 LF, drew $7.30, $10.00 and $10.00 per LF; on 11-430674, 3,660 LF drew $3.60 to $10.30. Those items were edging products rather than poured concrete. They still show the range GCs and agencies see for a border between areas.</p>`,
  watch: `<p>Concrete has minimums: plant short-load fees, a pump if the truck can't reach, a finisher for a half day even for 20 LF. Group small pours together, and price pump or wheelbarrow access when trucks can't get close.</p>`,
  src: "Caltrans Bid Summaries 11-431064 item 36 and 11-430674 item 47."
},

{ h: "DG paving, pavers and stepping stones",
  lead: "These look simple. The specs often make them engineered surfaces with base, compaction, binder and testing.",
  body: `<p><strong>Real spec, City of San Diego K-24-1952, Section 200-5 Stabilized Decomposed Granite Paving:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Soil sterilant on the subgrade, mixed into the top 2 to 4 inches</li>
<li>Subgrade compacted to at least 90%</li>
<li>Edging installed full depth around the DG</li>
<li>DG pre-blended with organic binder at the manufacturer's rate, “not less than of twelve (12) pounds of Organic Binder per one (1) ton” of DG; drop-spreading binder or roto-tilling it in “is not acceptable”</li>
<li>Placed “in two (2) equal two-inch (2") lifts”</li>
<li>Finished surface within 1/8 inch in 10 feet, accessible per code</li>
<li>Compaction test by a certified lab for each lift, at the contractor's cost</li>
</ul>
<div class="twocol">
<div><h3>Pavers</h3><ul>
<li>Area by pattern; base rock CY, bedding sand CY, edge restraint LF</li>
<li>Cuts along curves add labor and waste</li>
<li>Permeable pavers add open-graded rock layers and often an underdrain</li>
</ul></div>
<div><h3>Stepping stones</h3><ul>
<li>Count by size (EA); sand or DG bed per detail</li>
<li>Set flush or 1/2 inch above grade as the detail shows</li>
<li>Heavy flagstones may need two workers or a machine</li>
</ul></div>
</div>`,
  watch: `<p>Real Caltrans prices for plain DG: 11-430674 “Decomposed Granite,” 55,900 SQFT, bids $1.70 to $3.00 per SQFT. A small stabilized DG path with two lifts, binder, sterilant and lab tests costs much more per SF. Never copy a big-job unit price onto a small path.</p>`,
  src: "City of San Diego K-24-1952-DBB-3-A, 200-5.3.2, 200-5.3.3 and 200-5.3.4; Caltrans 11-430674 Bid Summary item 45."
},

{ h: "Worked example · A stabilized DG path",
  lead: "1,500 SF of stabilized DG, 4 inches thick in two lifts, steel edging along 200 LF, built to the City spec above.",
  exLabel: "Practice example (made-up prices, conversions and rates; get supplier tons-per-CY and a lab test price)",
  ex: `<ul>
<li>Compacted volume: 1,500 × 4 ÷ 324 = 6,000 ÷ 324 = <strong>18.5 CY</strong></li>
<li>Compaction allowance (example 20%): 18.5 × 1.20 = <strong>22.2 CY loose</strong></li>
<li>Weight (example 1.4 tons per CY from the supplier): 22.2 × 1.4 = <strong>31.1 tons</strong></li>
<li>Binder check: 31.1 tons × 12 lb = <strong>373 lb minimum</strong> (the pre-blended product must meet this)</li>
<li>Lab tests: 2 lifts = <strong>2 test visits</strong> (more if the path is placed on different days)</li>
</ul>
<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Cost line</th><th>Math</th><th class="num">Cost</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Pre-blended stabilized DG (example $95/ton delivered)</td><td>31.1 × $95</td><td class="num">$2,954.50</td></tr>
<tr><td>Steel edging (example $4.50/LF installed material)</td><td>200 × $4.50</td><td class="num">$900.00</td></tr>
<tr><td>Labor: prep, sterilant, place, roll (example 0.05 LH/SF)</td><td>1,500 × 0.05 = 75 LH × $36.00</td><td class="num">$2,700.00</td></tr>
<tr><td>Roller rental (example)</td><td>2 days × $200</td><td class="num">$400.00</td></tr>
<tr><td>Compaction tests (example $350 per visit)</td><td>2 × $350</td><td class="num">$700.00</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Direct cost</strong></td><td>2,954.50 + 900 + 2,700 + 400 + 700</td><td class="num"><strong>$7,654.50</strong></td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>Per SF: $7,654.50 ÷ 1,500 = <strong>$5.10/SF</strong>, about twice the top real Caltrans bid for plain DG on a 55,900 SF job.</p>`,
  body: `<p>The tests and the second lift are what make this path expensive. Readers who skip the spec and price “DG, 4 inches” at a plain-DG unit price lose the difference.</p>`
},

{ h: "Walls: seat walls vs. retaining walls",
  lead: "Low garden and seat walls can fit a landscape scope. Retaining walls hold back soil and usually need engineering, permits and a concrete or masonry contractor.",
  body: `<div class="twocol">
<div><h3>Seat walls and low garden walls</h3><ul>
<li>Decorative or for sitting; not holding back much soil</li>
<li>Still need a footing, rebar and a cap per detail</li>
<li>Often CMU block with stone or stucco veneer, or cast concrete</li>
<li>Take off by LF and height, footing CY, block count, cap LF</li>
</ul></div>
<div><h3>Retaining walls</h3><ul>
<li>Hold back soil; failure can hurt people and property</li>
<li>Designed by an engineer; taller walls or walls with loads above them need building permits</li>
<li>Need wall drains, waterproofing, backfill compaction and inspections</li>
<li>Usually C-8 or C-29 work; sub it or exclude it</li>
</ul></div>
</div>`,
  ex: `<p>Real example, 11-4C0034 (San Ysidro gateway, bid May 11, 2023, license required: Class A or a combination of C-8, C-10 and C-27 making up the majority of the work). Items 53–55 were “Structural Concrete, Wall” 175 CY, “Concrete Surface Texture” 600 SQFT and “Bar Reinforcing Steel” 11,350 LB. The low bidder priced the wall concrete at $2,200 per CY: $385,000 of a $1,597,008.50 bid. Diversified Landscape Co., bidding as prime, listed Hazard Construction for 100% of items 53, 54 and 55. Hazard, which won as prime, listed Marina Landscape for the landscape items.</p>`,
  watch: `<p>Wall permit rules vary by city and by wall height and load. Never assume a wall is small enough to skip a permit. If the plans show a wall without structural details, send an RFI. If your proposal includes any wall, name the drawing, the height and the detail you priced.</p>`,
  src: "Caltrans 11-4C0034 Special Provisions (Notice to Bidders, license requirement) and Bid Summary pages 1, 4 and S1–S2 (subcontractor lists); CSLB C-8 and C-29 classification pages."
},

{ h: "Boulders and decorative rock",
  lead: "Boulders are a landscape item, but they're heavy equipment work with submittals, placement approvals and big price swings.",
  body: `<ul>
<li><strong>Take off</strong> by size group (EA). Module 7 covers counting.</li>
<li><strong>Price</strong> the boulder, delivery, a machine big enough to set it, and rigging.</li>
<li><strong>Approval time:</strong> photos before delivery, an inspector picking the face and orientation.</li>
<li><strong>Base:</strong> compacted subgrade, bury depth per detail.</li>
</ul>`,
  ex: `<p>Real spec, 11-422004 section 20-5.06: submit the boulder source and photos of the front, back and side of each boulder at least 30 days before delivery; boulders tan, rounded, no blast holes, from named quarries “or equal”; sizes A (5×4×3 ft) to D (2×2×1 ft); final location and orientation authorized at least 5 business days before installation; compact the subgrade to 90%; bury about 1/3 of the height. The 20 “Decorative Boulders” (item 19) drew bids of $320, $330, $620 and $1,250 each.</p>`,
  watch: `<p>A boulder that's twice as long is about eight times as heavy. Size letters on the plan decide whether a skid steer works or you need a loader or crane truck. Get the weight from the quarry and check your machine's lift capacity.</p>`,
  src: "11-422004 Special Provisions 20-5.06A–20-5.06C; Bid Summary item 19."
},

{ h: "Site furnishings and amenities",
  lead: "Benches, trash and recycling receptacles, bike racks, picnic tables and drinking fountains often sit in the landscape spec. Price the install from the detail, and the product from a written supplier quote.",
  body: `<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Mount type</th><th>What the work is</th><th>Watch for</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Embedded</td><td>Legs set in concrete footings</td><td>Footing size, holding the item level while concrete sets</td></tr>
<tr><td>Surface mount</td><td>Bolted to existing or new concrete</td><td>Anchors, drilling, pad must exist first</td></tr>
<tr><td>Freestanding</td><td>Set in place, maybe chained</td><td>Weight, delivery, theft</td></tr>
<tr><td>On a new pad</td><td>Concrete pad under the item</td><td>Who pours the pad: you or the concrete sub</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>`,
  ex: `<p>Real example, City of San Diego K-24-1952, Section 206-9 Site Furnishings: picnic table, bench, litter and recycling receptacle, dog clean-up station and ADA accessible route guide sign, each with submittals and installation requirements. All of them are paid inside the lump sum “Construction of Park Improvements,” together with walkways, irrigation, planting and lighting (Section 7-3.1). A landscape sub quoting this job must say which furnishings it installs.</p>`,
  watch: `<p>Specified furnishings often have long lead times and freight from out of state. Get the supplier's written quote with lead time, freight and whether hardware and anchors are included. Then decide: “Furnish and install,” “Install only, owner-furnished,” or “Excluded.”</p>`,
  deeper: `<p>The D-34 Prefabricated Equipment subcategory lists playground equipment and bus stop shelters. Setting a bench or a trash receptacle on a footing as part of landscape work is common for landscape contractors; if a contract is mostly furnishings, ask CSLB which class fits.</p>`,
  src: "City of San Diego K-24-1952-DBB-3-A, 206-9 and 7-3.1; CSLB C-61/D-34 classification."
},

{ h: "Playgrounds, fencing, gates and shade structures",
  lead: "These are specialty trades with their own safety rules and licenses. Most landscape subs exclude them or sub them out.",
  body: `<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Scope</th><th>Who usually does it</th><th>What to know</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Playground equipment</td><td>Manufacturer's certified installer</td><td>D-34 lists playground equipment; C-13 lists playground game equipment</td></tr>
<tr><td>Safety surfacing (poured rubber, engineered wood fiber, synthetic turf with pad)</td><td>Surfacing specialist</td><td>Must meet the playground standards; depth and fall heights set by the design</td></tr>
<tr><td>Fencing and gates</td><td>Fence contractor (C-13)</td><td>C-13 covers fences, railings, game court enclosures, backstops and gates, not masonry walls</td></tr>
<tr><td>Ornamental metal railings and gates</td><td>C-23 or C-13</td><td>Shop drawings, welding, finish</td></tr>
<tr><td>Shade structures, pergolas</td><td>B contractor or specialty with engineered design</td><td>Building permits, footings, inspections</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>`,
  deeper: `<p>California Health and Safety Code §115725 says new public playgrounds must meet the American Society for Testing and Materials (ASTM) playground standards and the U.S. Consumer Product Safety Commission guidelines, and that operators of public playgrounds must have a playground safety inspector certified by the National Playground Safety Institute do an initial inspection. That's why owners want the equipment maker's installer, and why most landscape subs write “playground equipment and safety surfacing by others.”</p>`,
  ex: `<p>Real example, City of San Diego K-24-1952 includes Section 201-12 Precast Concrete Fence (built per the 2016 California Building Code, panels and posts at 4,500 psi) and Section 206-8 Service Access Gate. Both are fence-and-structure work a landscape sub would normally exclude.</p>`,
  src: "Cal. Health & Safety Code §115725 (text checked at california.public.law); CSLB C-13, C-23 and C-61/D-34 classification pages; City K-24-1952 201-12 and 206-8."
},

{ h: "Landscape lighting and electrical",
  lead: "Line-voltage electrical work belongs to a C-10 electrical contractor. Low-voltage landscape lighting is listed by name under the C-7. Plan to sub electrical, not to do it.",
  body: `<div class="twocol">
<div><h3>Line voltage (120V and up)</h3><ul>
<li>Power to irrigation controllers</li>
<li>Pole lights, bollards, uplights on 120V or 277V</li>
<li>Conduit, pull boxes, wire, panels, transformers</li>
<li>Needs an electrical permit and inspection</li>
<li>Do it with a C-10 sub, or exclude it</li>
</ul></div>
<div><h3>Low voltage (energy-limited)</h3><ul>
<li>12V landscape lighting fed by a transformer</li>
<li>The transformer plugs into or is wired to a 120V circuit (C-10 work)</li>
<li>CSLB's C-7 description lists “low voltage landscape lighting” by name</li>
<li>The C-27 text does not mention lighting; ask CSLB before you self-perform it</li>
</ul></div>
</div>`,
  ex: `<p>Real examples: on 11-428704, the first three bidders (ACCU, Diversified Landscape and Griffith) all listed a subcontractor for “Electric Service for Irrigation” (item 69). Bids for that one item ran from $7,000 to $25,000. On 11-4C0034, Diversified listed a subcontractor (Fortte Construction) for “Modifying Lighting Systems,” and the winning bidder listed Siege Electric for 100% of item 60, priced at $170,000 in the low bid. Landscape companies sub the electrical, even when they're the prime.</p>`,
  watch: `<p>The irrigation controller needs power before you can test and program it. If the electrician isn't scheduled, you can't finish. In your proposal: “120V power, conduit and final connection to controller(s) by electrical contractor. We provide the controller and coordinate its location.”</p>`,
  src: "CSLB C-10 and C-7 classification pages; Caltrans 11-428704 Bid Summary item 69 and subcontractor lists (see Module 15); 11-4C0034 Bid Summary pages 4 and S1–S3."
},

{ h: "Synthetic turf and sports fields",
  lead: "Synthetic turf is base work plus a product install. Sports fields add drainage, testing and certified installers.",
  body: `<ul>
<li><strong>Layers:</strong> compacted subgrade, sometimes geotextile, crushed aggregate base (often 3–4 inches), fine leveling layer, the turf, seams, infill.</li>
<li><strong>Takeoff:</strong> turf SF (add seam and cut waste per roll width), base CY = SF × inches ÷ 324 compacted, edge board or nailer LF, infill by pounds per SF from the manufacturer.</li>
<li><strong>Sports fields:</strong> engineered drainage under the field, shock pads, line markings, and impact tests. The turf maker's certified installer usually does the install.</li>
<li><strong>Licensing:</strong> CSLB's D-12 Synthetic Products description lists “synthetic turf” by name. Many C-27 firms install turf in landscape areas. For a turf-only or sports-field contract, check what the bid notice requires and ask CSLB if unsure.</li>
</ul>`,
  ex: `<p><strong>Base math (practice example):</strong> 3,000 SF of turf on 4" of base. 3,000 × 4 ÷ 324 = <strong>37.0 CY compacted</strong>. With an example 25% compaction allowance: 37.0 × 1.25 = <strong>46.3 CY</strong> to order. Turf on 15-ft rolls (example) with 8% waste: 3,000 × 1.08 = <strong>3,240 SF</strong> to buy.</p>`,
  watch: `<p>Turf warranties often require the maker's approved installer. If you sub it, get the installer's warranty to pass through to the owner, and say so in your proposal.</p>`,
  src: "CSLB C-61/D-12 Synthetic Products classification (cslb.ca.gov)."
},

{ h: "Water features, pools and fountains",
  lead: "Water features combine plumbing, pumps, electrical, waterproofing and stone. They're high-risk, high-callback work. Most landscape subs exclude them or sub them to a specialist.",
  body: `<ul>
<li><strong>Swimming pools and spas</strong> are C-53 work. Exclude them.</li>
<li><strong>Fountains and recirculating features</strong> need pumps, piping, a basin or liner, filtration, an electrical circuit (C-10), and often a building permit. The owner may want a fountain specialist with a warranty.</li>
<li><strong>Pondless or simple rock features</strong> are sometimes built by landscape firms. Price them only with a full detail, a pump schedule and a written power plan.</li>
<li><strong>Your part, even when excluded:</strong> boulders, planting and irrigation around the feature, and sleeves for its pipe and power under paving.</li>
</ul>`,
  watch: `<p>Leaks show up after you leave. If you take on a water feature, read the warranty section: a one-year warranty on a leaking basin can cost more than the whole item. Proposal wording: “Water feature construction, pumps, filtration, waterproofing and electrical excluded. Includes boulders, planting and irrigation around the feature per L-series.”</p>`,
  src: "CSLB C-53 Swimming Pool and C-10 Electrical classification pages."
},

{ h: "Water meter, point of connection and fees",
  lead: "Irrigation starts at a water source. On public jobs the meter and its fees can be a large bid item that is mostly a pass-through.",
  body: `<ul>
<li><strong>Point of connection (POC):</strong> where your irrigation work begins. Module 6 covers the takeoff.</li>
<li><strong>Water meter:</strong> usually set by the water agency after the owner or contractor pays fees. Capacity fees for a new irrigation meter can be large.</li>
<li><strong>Service line:</strong> the pipe from the main to the meter. Often the agency's work or a civil sub's (tapping a public main is not landscape work).</li>
<li><strong>Water service charges:</strong> the monthly bill for water during construction and plant establishment.</li>
</ul>`,
  ex: `<p>Real example, 11-431064 (SR-905 Otay Mesa, 2021). Special provision 5-1.20E lists the water meter charges: two 2" Otay Water District meters, each with a $210 connection fee, one with a $118,247 meter charge (the other covered by existing credits). The contractor must “make arrangements and pay the charges,” and Caltrans adjusts the lump sum if the charge changes. The three bidders priced “Water Meter Charges” at $118,000, $148,000 and $146,900. The winner bid $667 under the listed charges of $118,667 ($118,247 + $210 + $210). The same job listed City of San Diego water service at $78.72 per month plus $7.053 per HCF, and Otay at $240.85 per meter per month plus $5.84 per HCF. “Irrigation Water Service Charges” drew $60,000, $60,000 and $553,400.</p>`,
  watch: `<p>On a private job, write: “Water meter, meter and capacity fees, service line to the POC, and water usage by owner/GC. Irrigation work starts at the POC shown on L-1.” On a public prime bid, find the fee table in the specs and carry at least the listed amount, plus your time to arrange it.</p>`,
  src: "Caltrans 11-431064 Special Provisions 5-1.20E and 5-1.20F; Bid Summary items 67 and 68."
},

{ h: "Backflow, temporary water and sleeves",
  lead: "Three small items that often fall between trades. Each one needs a written owner.",
  body: `<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>What it is</th><th>Who usually does it</th><th>Watch for</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Backflow preventer</td><td>Assembly that stops irrigation water flowing back into drinking water</td><td>Landscape (irrigation) or plumbing</td><td>Test by a certified tester after install; enclosure, pad and freeze protection</td></tr>
<tr><td>Backflow enclosure</td><td>Locking cage or cover over the assembly</td><td>Landscape</td><td>Concrete pad by whom; color and model in specs</td></tr>
<tr><td>Temporary water</td><td>Water for dust, compaction, testing and early planting before the meter is live</td><td>GC on private jobs; prime on public jobs</td><td>Hydrant meter deposit and rental, hauling water</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sleeves under paving</td><td>Empty pipes under walks and drives for later irrigation pipe and wire</td><td>Landscape, before paving</td><td>Must go in months before planting; mark ends</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>`,
  ex: `<p>Real examples: 11-430674 and 11-431064 each had a “2" Backflow Preventer Assembly” item (EA); 11-431064's three bidders priced it at $7,000, $8,100 and $9,000 each. 11-422004 had “Certify Existing Backflow Preventers” (LS), priced from $275 to $2,200. The City of San Diego's Fire Hydrant Meter Program (included in K-24-1952, Appendix B) says hydrant meters are for temporary use of no longer than 12 months, connect with a 2½" swivel, must have a reduced pressure principle backflow assembly, and require a refundable deposit.</p>`,
  watch: `<p>Sleeves are the most common “we forgot” item. The paving sub pours in month 3, you show up in month 8, and there's no way under the walk. Price boring or sawcutting if sleeves weren't installed, or say: “Sleeves by others per our sleeve layout, installed before paving.”</p>`,
  src: "Caltrans Bid Summaries 11-431064 item 46, 11-430674 item 72, 11-422004 item 32; City of San Diego K-24-1952 Appendix B (Water Department Instruction DI 55.27, Fire Hydrant Meter Program), sections 3.2, 4.1 and 4.2."
},

{ h: "DigAlert (811), potholing and subsurface locating",
  lead: "Before anyone digs, California law requires marking the dig area and calling the regional notification center. In Southern California that's DigAlert, reached by calling 811.",
  body: `<ol class="steps">
<li><div><b>Delineate</b>Mark the area you'll dig in white (Gov. Code §4216 defines “delineate” as marking in white). Utility owners may refuse to locate an area that isn't delineated.</div></li>
<li class="hl"><div><b>Call or go online at least 2 working days, and not more than 14 calendar days, before digging</b>The day you call doesn't count (Gov. Code §4216.2(b)).</div></li>
<li><div><b>Keep the ticket alive</b>A ticket is valid for 28 days. Renew it by the end of day 28 if work continues (§4216.2(d)).</div></li>
<li><div><b>Hand dig in the tolerance zone</b>The tolerance zone is 24 inches on each side of a utility mark. Find the exact location with hand tools before using power equipment there (§4216 and §4216.4(a)).</div></li>
<li><div><b>Locate private lines yourself</b>DigAlert notifies utility owners. It does not locate the owner's private irrigation, lighting or drain lines on site. Hire a private locator when the plans show existing private utilities.</div></li>
</ol>`,
  ex: `<p>Real example: several Caltrans jobs carry a “Subsurface Locator” item (LS). On 11-431064, special provision 87-1.03X requires certified operators, two different locating technologies (one must be ground penetrating radar or subsurface imaging), marks maintained until the work is done, and GPS data for each facility found. Bids: $9,400, $11,000 and $25,800 (11-431064 item 65); on 11-428804, $1,300 to $60,819.75.</p>`,
  watch: `<p>Potholing (digging small test holes, often by vacuum excavation, to see a utility's exact depth) costs time and money. Price it wherever your trench crosses marked utilities, and price slower trenching inside tolerance zones. Module 9 covers the production loss.</p>`,
  src: "Cal. Gov. Code §§4216, 4216.2 and 4216.4 (text checked at california.public.law; official text at leginfo.legislature.ca.gov); Caltrans 11-431064 Special Provisions 87-1.01D(3) and 87-1.03X; Bid Summaries 11-431064 item 65 and 11-428804 item 98."
},

{ h: "Plant establishment, maintenance and weed abatement",
  lead: "After planting comes a care period: often 90 days on private and City jobs, and hundreds of working days on Caltrans jobs. It's landscape work, and it's priced by time.",
  body: `<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Real job</th><th>Establishment period</th><th class="num">Bids for “Plant Establishment Work” (LS)</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>City of San Diego K-24-1952</td><td>90 calendar days, inside the 270 working day contract time</td><td class="num">Inside the lump sum</td></tr>
<tr><td>Caltrans 11-428704</td><td>250 working days</td><td class="num">$10,000 – $300,000</td></tr>
<tr><td>Caltrans 11-431064</td><td>750 working days</td><td class="num">$95,000 – $153,900</td></tr>
<tr><td>Caltrans 11-430674</td><td>750 working days</td><td class="num">$153,000 – $510,000</td></tr>
<tr><td>Caltrans 11-4C0034</td><td>Plant establishment after 110 working days</td><td class="num">$10,000 (low bid)</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<ul>
<li><strong>Weed abatement:</strong> killing and removing weeds before work starts, and keeping them out during establishment. Module 7 covers weed germination cycles.</li>
<li><strong>Long-term maintenance (1-year, 3-year):</strong> sometimes an alternate or a separate maintenance contract. Price it as its own line with monthly visits.</li>
</ul>`,
  ex: `<p><strong>Worked example (practice numbers):</strong> 90-day establishment on a 20,000 SF park planting.</p>
<ul>
<li>Weekly visits: 13 weeks × 2 workers × 4 hours = 104 LH × $36.00 = <strong>$3,744.00</strong></li>
<li>Truck and fuel (example $60 per visit): 13 × $60 = <strong>$780.00</strong></li>
<li>Plant replacement allowance (example 3% of $12,000 plant material): <strong>$360.00</strong></li>
<li>Fertilizer and weed control materials (example): <strong>$400.00</strong></li>
<li>Total: 3,744 + 780 + 360 + 400 = <strong>$5,284.00</strong>, or $5,284 ÷ 3 = <strong>$1,761.33 per month</strong></li>
</ul>`,
  watch: `<p>Check who pays for water during establishment, whether the period can be extended for dead plants or poor work, and whether your final payment or retention waits for its end. Module 5 and Module 16 cover the spec and the management side.</p>`,
  src: "City K-24-1952 Attachment A item 3 and 6-1.1 item 4; Caltrans Bid Summaries 11-428704 item 30, 11-431064 item 31, 11-430674 item 40, 11-4C0034 item 31; contract days from the Real Bid Lab data (realbids.js)."
},

{ h: "General conditions: traffic, mobilization, testing and more",
  lead: "When you bid as prime, these are line items with real money. When you bid as a sub, they're usually the GC's, and your proposal should say so.",
  body: `<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>What it covers</th><th>Real bid range (job, item)</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Traffic Control System (LS)</td><td>Lane closures, cones, signs, flaggers, arrow boards</td><td>$63,067.34 – $350,000 (11-428804 #6)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Construction Area Signs (LS)</td><td>Advance warning signs for the work zone</td><td>$22,500 – $75,000 (11-428804 #5)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Mobilization (LS)</td><td>Moving in, setup, bonds, insurance, startup costs</td><td>$20,000 – $330,000 (11-428704 #70)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Job Site Management (LS)</td><td>Housekeeping, spill prevention, waste and materials management</td><td>$5,000 – $125,000 (11-428704 #9)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Manage Solid Waste Generated by the Public (HR)</td><td>Picking up trash and debris left by the public</td><td>$81 – $345 per HR (11-428704 #18)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Testing and inspection</td><td>Compaction, concrete, soil, backflow, pressure tests</td><td>Often inside other items; read who pays</td></tr>
<tr><td>Dewatering</td><td>Pumping groundwater out of excavations</td><td>Rare on landscape; exclude unless shown</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>`,
  ex: `<p>Real subcontracting pattern: on 11-4C0034, the second bidder listed a traffic control firm for item 3 (Construction Area Signs), 86% of item 4 (Traffic Control System), and the sign items. On 11-422004, the winning landscape firm listed a sign company for the traffic items and an environmental firm for the lead compliance plan, the water pollution control program and part of job site management. Landscape primes sub these out all the time.</p>`,
  watch: `<p>As a sub, don't let these slip into your scope through vague wording like “all work required for a complete installation.” Module 10 explains how to price mobilization and time-related overhead when you're prime, and Module 13 lists the standard exclusions.</p>`,
  src: "Caltrans Bid Summaries 11-428804 items 5–6; 11-428704 items 9, 18, 70; 11-4C0034 and 11-422004 subcontractor lists."
},

{ h: "Bid day · Reading the scope sheet: NIC, by others and plugs",
  lead: "GCs send a scope sheet or bid form with the invitation. Learn its words, then answer every line.",
  body: `<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Word</th><th>Meaning</th><th>What you do</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>NIC</td><td>Not in contract: nobody on this job does it</td><td>Don't price it; don't exclude it as if it were yours</td></tr>
<tr><td>By others</td><td>Another trade or the owner does it</td><td>Coordinate with it; make sure your price doesn't depend on it silently</td></tr>
<tr><td>OFCI</td><td>Owner-furnished, contractor-installed</td><td>Price install, receiving, storage; not the product</td></tr>
<tr><td>Alternate</td><td>A priced option the owner may add or delete</td><td>Price exactly the alternate scope, separately</td></tr>
<tr><td>Allowance</td><td>A fixed amount the owner sets for an item not fully designed</td><td>Carry the exact amount; price labor if the allowance is material only</td></tr>
<tr><td>Plug number</td><td>A placeholder price used when a real quote isn't in yet</td><td>Use only when needed, write down where it came from, replace it fast</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<ol class="steps">
<li><div><b>Number every line</b>Copy the GC's scope items into your estimate in the same order.</div></li>
<li class="hl"><div><b>Answer each one</b>Included, included by named sub, excluded, or NIC.</div></li>
<li><div><b>Match the bid form</b>If the GC asks for breakouts (demo, grading, drainage, hardscape), give them. GCs level bids line by line.</div></li>
</ol>`,
  watch: `<p>When you exclude an item, the GC adds a plug for it to your number while leveling. If that plug is bigger than what it would have cost you to include the item, your low bid isn't low anymore. Exclude what truly belongs to others (Module 13).</p>`
},

{ h: "Bid day · Subbing out: quotes, licenses and markup",
  lead: "When you carry another trade's work, you take on its risk. Get it in writing, check the license, and mark it up.",
  body: `<ol class="steps">
<li><div><b>Ask early</b>Send the sub the plans, specs, addenda and a written scope one to two weeks before bid day. Module 8's quote request works for subs too.</div></li>
<li><div><b>Check the license</b>Right class, active, bonded, workers' comp on CSLB's Instant License Check. On public work, DIR registration too.</div></li>
<li><div><b>Read the quote's exclusions</b>A concrete quote that excludes sawcutting, testing and pump time just moved those costs to you.</div></li>
<li class="hl"><div><b>Mark it up</b>You pay the sub whether or not the GC pays you. You coordinate, supervise and warranty their work.</div></li>
<li><div><b>List them when required</b>On public jobs, subs over one half of one percent of the prime's bid must be listed (Module 14).</div></li>
</ol>`,
  ex: `<p><strong>Worked example (practice numbers):</strong> a lighting and controller-power quote from a C-10 sub.</p>
<ul>
<li>Sub quote: <strong>$18,500.00</strong></li>
<li>Your coordination and supervision (example 8 hr × $55.00): <strong>$440.00</strong></li>
<li>Markup on the sub (example 8%): $18,500 × 0.08 = <strong>$1,480.00</strong></li>
<li>Total carried in your bid: 18,500 + 440 + 1,480 = <strong>$20,420.00</strong></li>
</ul>
<p>If the GC could hire the same electrician directly for $18,500, your $20,420 might lose the item. That's a reason to ask the GC whether they want lighting in your package at all.</p>`,
  watch: `<p>Sub quotes that arrive in the last hour are often incomplete. Compare at least two when you can, and read every exclusion line before you use the lower one.</p>`,
  src: "Listing threshold: City K-24-1952 Instructions to Bidders item 12.1 (subcontractors over 0.5% of the total bid, per the Subletting and Subcontracting Fair Practices Act)."
},

{ h: "Bid day · When to decline a scope",
  lead: "Saying no to part of a bid is a skill. Decline a scope when the risk is bigger than the reward or when it isn't yours to do.",
  body: `<div class="twocol">
<div><h3>Decline or exclude when</h3><ul>
<li>Your license doesn't cover it and you have no qualified sub</li>
<li>You have no written price and can't get one before bid day</li>
<li>It needs engineering you don't have (retaining walls, structures)</li>
<li>It carries open-ended risk (hazardous soils, dewatering, unknown demo)</li>
<li>It would tie up your crews on work you're slow at</li>
</ul></div>
<div><h3>Consider including when</h3><ul>
<li>It's small and inside your planting areas</li>
<li>It's on the landscape sheets and the GC won't level without it</li>
<li>You have a trusted sub with a written quote</li>
<li>Including it makes your bid easier to compare and more likely to win</li>
<li>On a public prime bid where every item must be covered</li>
</ul></div>
</div>
<p><strong>How to say no politely:</strong> “Our proposal covers the irrigation, planting, fine grading and landscape drainage on L-1 through L-8. We are not including the retaining walls on C-5 or the site lighting on E-1. We're happy to help coordinate with the wall and electrical contractors.”</p>`,
  watch: `<p>Don't decline by silence. If the GC's form has a line for “site lighting” and you leave it blank, they may read blank as “included at zero.” Write “Excluded” on the line.</p>`
},

{ h: "Scenario 1 · A private GC's scope sheet",
  lead: "A retail center GC sends a landscape scope sheet: demo existing planters, fine grading, area drains, concrete mow curbs, site furnishings, landscape lighting, plant establishment 90 days.",
  body: `<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Line</th><th>Right move</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Demo existing planters</td><td>Include, with assumed thickness and a unit price for extra</td><td>Small, in your areas, you can price it</td></tr>
<tr><td>Fine grading</td><td>Include, with the rough grade tolerance clarification</td><td>Your work; the hand-off protects you</td></tr>
<tr><td>Area drains</td><td>Include drains and pipe in planters; exclude connection to storm drain</td><td>Landscape drainage yes, civil storm drain no</td></tr>
<tr><td>Concrete mow curbs</td><td>Include</td><td>Small concrete in the landscape</td></tr>
<tr><td>Site furnishings</td><td>Include install from a written supplier quote, or install-only if owner-furnished</td><td>Clear product and lead time</td></tr>
<tr><td>Landscape lighting</td><td>Sub to a C-10 with markup, or exclude after asking the GC</td><td>Not your license</td></tr>
<tr><td>Establishment 90 days</td><td>Include as its own line</td><td>Landscape work priced by time</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>`,
  watch: `<p>The wrong move is to send one lump sum with “per plans and specs.” The GC will assume every line is in, including the lighting you never priced.</p>`
},

{ h: "Scenario 2 · A City of San Diego park, lump sum",
  lead: "You're quoting the landscape to A-licensed primes on a City park job like K-24-1952: one lump sum item covers walkways, seating, irrigation, planting and lighting, and clearing and grubbing includes sawcutting and demolition.",
  body: `<ol class="steps">
<li><div><b>Confirm you can't be prime</b>The notice says the prime must hold an A license. You bid to A contractors (Module 2).</div></li>
<li><div><b>Split the lump sum</b>Write your own list from the plans: irrigation, planting, soil prep, finish grading, stabilized DG, furnishings, establishment. Note what you won't do: walkways, curb ramps, lighting, fence, gate.</div></li>
<li class="hl"><div><b>Find the buried demo</b>Section 300-1.1 puts the removal of “concrete mow curb,” “concrete pavers,” vegetation and other improvements inside clearing and grubbing. Ask each prime whether removals in planting areas are yours, and price them as a separate line they can accept or not.</div></li>
<li><div><b>Read the special specs</b>Stabilized DG has two lifts, binder minimums and lab tests at your cost. Finish grading needs 1% slope and Resident Engineer approval. Establishment is 90 calendar days inside the contract time.</div></li>
<li><div><b>Send a clear quote</b>Line items, inclusions, exclusions, and the SLBE/ELBE status if you have it (Module 2).</div></li>
</ol>`,
  watch: `<p>Primes on lump sum jobs compare subs by what's included. A landscape quote that's $8,000 higher but includes the planter demo and DG testing can beat a lower quote that excludes them.</p>`,
  src: "City of San Diego K-24-1952-DBB-3-A: Notice Inviting Bids item 6; 7-3.1; 300-1.1; 200-5; 801-2.3; 6-1.1 item 4."
},

{ h: "Scenario 3 · A Caltrans job with walls and lighting",
  lead: "Caltrans 11-4C0034: “Replace gateway sign, install landscape, hardscape, & accent lighting.” Engineer's estimate $2,176,430. License: Class A, or a combination of C-8, C-10 and C-27 making up the majority of the work.",
  body: `<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Bidder</th><th class="num">Bid</th><th>Listed subs (from the bid summary)</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Hazard Construction (won)</td><td class="num">$1,597,008.50</td><td>Marina Landscape for items 19, 20–49, 51, 52, 64 (landscape and irrigation); Siege Electric for item 60 (lighting); a rebar sub for item 55; a sign and traffic firm</td></tr>
<tr><td>Diversified Landscape (2nd)</td><td class="num">$2,026,050.15</td><td>Hazard Construction for items 53, 54, 55 (wall concrete, texture, rebar); a traffic and sign firm; Fortte Construction for lighting</td></tr>
<tr><td>FEC Future Contractors (3rd)</td><td class="num">$2,798,036.25</td><td>Siege Electric for item 60; a wall subcontractor for part of item 53</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p><strong>The lesson:</strong> the same pieces were bought by different primes. The civil contractor subbed the landscape; the landscape contractor subbed the wall. Both were legal ways to bid. The difference was price: the wall alone was $385,000 in the low bid.</p>`,
  watch: `<p>Before you bid a job like this as prime, you need a wall sub and an electrical sub with written quotes. Before you bid it as a sub, find out which primes are bidding (the planholders list) and send each one your landscape price.</p>`,
  src: "Caltrans 11-4C0034 Special Provisions (Notice to Bidders) and Bid Summary, pages 1, 4 and S1–S3 (bid opening 05/11/2023)."
},

{ h: "Scenario 4 · Halfway through the job",
  lead: "You won a private job. The rough grade in the shrub areas is 3 inches high, the sleeves weren't installed, and there's no power at the controller.",
  body: `<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Problem</th><th>What your proposal said</th><th>Result</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Grade 3" high</td><td>“Rough grade delivered to ±0.1 ft of finish grade less mulch and amendment depth, by others”</td><td>Change order for export (the $5,452 example)</td></tr>
<tr><td>No sleeves</td><td>“Sleeves by others per our layout, installed before paving”</td><td>Change order for boring under the walk</td></tr>
<tr><td>No controller power</td><td>“120V power and final connection by electrical”</td><td>GC schedules the electrician; your schedule extends</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>Now imagine the same proposal with none of those lines. All three problems become yours, at your cost, with no extra time.</p>`,
  watch: `<p>Write it down when it happens: photos, dates, a short email to the GC the same day, before you do the extra work. Module 16 covers change orders and time-and-material tickets.</p>`
},

{ h: "Common mistakes",
  lead: "Every one of these has cost landscape companies real money. Each has a simple fix.",
  body: `<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Mistake</th><th>What it costs</th><th>How to avoid it</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Silent on items in the scope sheet</td><td>GC assumes they're included</td><td>Answer every line: included, sub, excluded, NIC</td></tr>
<tr><td>Missing demo buried in another item</td><td>Unpaid removals and haul</td><td>Search specs for “remove,” “clearing and grubbing,” “in conflict with”</td></tr>
<tr><td>No rough grade tolerance</td><td>Paying to export soil</td><td>State the hand-off grade in every proposal</td></tr>
<tr><td>Forgetting swell when hauling</td><td>Short on trucks and dump fees</td><td>Bank × swell = loose; round loads up</td></tr>
<tr><td>Copying a big-job unit price to a small job</td><td>Loses minimums and setup</td><td>Price small items from crew days and minimum charges</td></tr>
<tr><td>Pricing BMP installs but not upkeep</td><td>Losses every rain</td><td>Add inspections, repairs and removal</td></tr>
<tr><td>Doing electrical or walls without the right license</td><td>Citations, payment disputes</td><td>Sub to C-10, C-8, C-29 or exclude</td></tr>
<tr><td>Using a sub quote without reading its exclusions</td><td>Gaps you pay for</td><td>Read every line; ask what's missing</td></tr>
<tr><td>Forgetting testing costs</td><td>Lab fees and retests</td><td>Find “testing” in every spec section you price</td></tr>
<tr><td>No DigAlert ticket or potholing in the price</td><td>Utility damage, delays</td><td>811 two working days ahead; price hand digging near marks</td></tr>
<tr><td>Assuming the meter and water are the owner's</td><td>Fees or water bills you didn't price</td><td>Read the fee tables; state it in writing</td></tr>
<tr><td>No markup on subs</td><td>Carrying their risk for free</td><td>Mark up every sub; add coordination time</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>`
},

{ h: "Reusable checklist · Inclusions and exclusions for other scopes",
  lead: "Go down this list for every bid. For each line, mark In, Sub, Out or N/A, and copy the matching sentence into your proposal. Adjust every sentence to the job.",
  body: `<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Scope</th><th>If included (sample wording)</th><th>If excluded (sample wording)</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Landscape demo</td><td>Removal and disposal of existing planting, irrigation and mulch in planting areas shown on [sheet]</td><td>Demolition of existing landscape and irrigation by others</td></tr>
<tr><td>Hard demo</td><td>Removal of [item] (approx. [qty] SF, [thickness]") with sawcut at edges to remain</td><td>Demolition of paving, curbs, walls and structures by others</td></tr>
<tr><td>Trees</td><td>Removal of [qty] trees and stump grinding to [depth]</td><td>Tree removal and tree protection by others</td></tr>
<tr><td>Hazardous material</td><td>(never include)</td><td>Testing, handling and disposal of hazardous or contaminated materials excluded</td></tr>
<tr><td>Rough grading</td><td>(rarely include)</td><td>Rough grade to ±0.1 ft of finish grade less amendment and mulch depth by others</td></tr>
<tr><td>Fine grading</td><td>Fine grading of planting areas per [details]</td><td>(normally yours)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Import / export</td><td>Import of planting soil at $___ per CY placed</td><td>Import and export of soil excluded</td></tr>
<tr><td>Staking and survey</td><td>Construction staking by licensed surveyor</td><td>Staking and grades by others</td></tr>
<tr><td>Testing</td><td>Testing listed in [spec section]</td><td>Third-party testing and inspection by others</td></tr>
<tr><td>SWPPP / BMPs</td><td>Install and maintain BMPs on [sheet] for [months]</td><td>SWPPP, QSD/QSP services, sampling and reporting by GC</td></tr>
<tr><td>Dust and sweeping</td><td>Dust control during our soil work</td><td>Dust control, water truck and street sweeping by GC</td></tr>
<tr><td>Landscape drainage</td><td>Area drains and pipe in planters to [point]</td><td>Storm drain, catch basins and connections by others</td></tr>
<tr><td>Bioretention</td><td>Engineered soil media and planting in basins</td><td>Basin excavation, liner, underdrain and structures by others</td></tr>
<tr><td>Concrete</td><td>Concrete mow curbs per [detail]</td><td>Site concrete, walks and curbs by others</td></tr>
<tr><td>DG, pavers, stone</td><td>[type] per [detail], including [testing/binder]</td><td>Paving and pavers by others</td></tr>
<tr><td>Walls</td><td>Seat walls per [detail] by licensed [class] sub</td><td>Retaining and site walls excluded</td></tr>
<tr><td>Furnishings</td><td>Furnish and install [items] per [spec]</td><td>Site furnishings by others / install only</td></tr>
<tr><td>Playground, fence, shade</td><td>By licensed sub per [spec]</td><td>Playground equipment, surfacing, fencing, gates and shade structures by others</td></tr>
<tr><td>Electrical</td><td>Lighting and controller power by licensed C-10 sub</td><td>120V power, conduit and connections by electrical</td></tr>
<tr><td>Water meter and POC</td><td>Connection at POC per [sheet]</td><td>Meter, fees, service line and water use by owner</td></tr>
<tr><td>Backflow</td><td>Backflow assembly, enclosure and test</td><td>Backflow by plumbing</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sleeves</td><td>Sleeves installed before paving when notified</td><td>Sleeves by others per our layout</td></tr>
<tr><td>Utilities</td><td>DigAlert and hand digging at marked utilities</td><td>Locating and repair of unmarked private utilities excluded</td></tr>
<tr><td>Establishment</td><td>[90]-day establishment period</td><td>Maintenance after the establishment period excluded</td></tr>
<tr><td>General conditions</td><td>(only as prime)</td><td>Traffic control, mobilization of others, permits, bonds and temporary facilities by GC</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>`,
  watch: `<p>Don't paste the whole list into every proposal. Use only lines that apply, and match the GC's scope sheet wording. Module 13 shows the full proposal format.</p>`
},

{ t: "terms", h: "Words to know",
  lead: "The words for the scopes next to landscape. Abbreviations are spelled out.",
  terms: [
    ["Scope sheet", "The GC's list of what each subcontractor's bid package must include."],
    ["NIC (not in contract)", "Work nobody on this contract does."],
    ["By others", "Work done by another trade, the GC or the owner."],
    ["OFCI (owner-furnished, contractor-installed)", "The owner buys the item; you install it."],
    ["Plug number", "A placeholder price used until a real quote arrives, or added by a GC for an excluded item."],
    ["Incidental and supplemental", "Work in another trade that is essential to finish your licensed work (B&P §7059, 16 CCR §831)."],
    ["Clearing and grubbing", "Removing vegetation, roots, stumps and debris before grading."],
    ["Roadside clearing", "Caltrans item for mowing, debris and weed removal along the work."],
    ["Sawcut", "A straight cut through concrete or asphalt so the part that stays has a clean edge."],
    ["Haul-off", "Loading and trucking removed material away from the site."],
    ["Tipping (dump) fee", "What a landfill or recycler charges per ton, load or yard."],
    ["Salvage", "Removing an item carefully and delivering it to the owner."],
    ["Protect in place", "Leaving an item and keeping it from damage during the work."],
    ["Rough grading", "Shaping the site to the grading plan with large equipment."],
    ["Fine (finish) grading", "Shaping planting areas to final grade, smooth and draining."],
    ["FG (finish grade)", "The final surface elevation shown on the plans."],
    ["Cut / fill", "Soil removed to lower grade / soil added to raise grade."],
    ["Import / export", "Soil brought onto / hauled off the site."],
    ["BCY (bank cubic yard)", "A cubic yard of soil in place before digging."],
    ["LCY (loose cubic yard)", "A cubic yard of dug, fluffed soil, as hauled."],
    ["CCY (compacted cubic yard)", "A cubic yard of soil after placing and compacting."],
    ["Swell / shrink factor", "How much soil grows when dug, and how much it shrinks when compacted."],
    ["Relative compaction", "Soil density as a percent of the lab maximum, like 90%."],
    ["Staking", "A surveyor's marks showing lines and grades in the field."],
    ["CGP (Construction General Permit)", "The State Water Board's storm water permit for construction disturbing 1 acre or more."],
    ["SWPPP (Storm Water Pollution Prevention Plan)", "The written storm water plan for a CGP project."],
    ["WPCP (Water Pollution Control Program/Plan)", "A shorter storm water plan for smaller jobs."],
    ["QSD / QSP", "Qualified SWPPP Developer (writes the plan) / Qualified SWPPP Practitioner (runs it in the field)."],
    ["BMP (best management practice)", "A single storm water control, like a fiber roll or inlet protection."],
    ["REAP (Rain Event Action Plan)", "A plan prepared before forecast rain on higher-risk sites."],
    ["Track-out", "Mud and dirt carried onto paved roads by tires (APCD Rule 55)."],
    ["Area drain", "A small grated inlet in a planter or lawn, piped away."],
    ["Subdrain / French drain", "A gravel trench with perforated pipe that collects underground water."],
    ["LID (Low Impact Development)", "Site design that soaks in or filters rain near where it falls."],
    ["Bioretention / biofiltration basin", "A planted basin that holds and filters storm water."],
    ["Engineered soil media", "A specified sand, compost and soil blend for biofiltration basins."],
    ["Mow curb", "A low concrete strip that separates turf from beds and gives the mower an edge."],
    ["Stabilized DG", "Decomposed granite blended with a binder and compacted into a firm path."],
    ["Retaining wall", "A wall that holds back soil; usually engineered and permitted."],
    ["POC (point of connection)", "Where the irrigation system connects to the water supply."],
    ["Hydrant meter", "A portable meter on a fire hydrant for temporary construction water."],
    ["DigAlert / 811", "Southern California's notification center for marking underground utilities before digging."],
    ["Tolerance zone", "24 inches on each side of a utility mark, where you must hand dig first."],
    ["Potholing", "Digging small test holes to find the exact depth of a utility."],
    ["PEP (plant establishment period)", "The care period after planting before final acceptance."]
  ]
},

{ t: "quiz", id: "m18", h: "Check yourself",
  lead: "Tap an answer to check it. Keep a calculator handy.",
  qs: [
    { q: "The C-27 description says a landscape contractor “prepares and grades plots and areas of land” for what?", a: ["Any construction project", "The installation of any architectural, horticultural and decorative treatment or arrangement", "Building pads and streets"], c: 1, why: "16 CCR §832.27 ties C-27 grading to landscape treatments, not to site or building grading." },
    { q: "Which license does CSLB's description list by name for “low voltage landscape lighting”?", a: ["C-7", "C-27", "C-13"], c: 0, why: "The C-7 Low Voltage Systems description lists low voltage landscape lighting; line voltage is C-10 work." },
    { q: "A City of San Diego spec says clearing and grubbing includes sawcutting and removal of mow curbs and pavers, paid in the lump sum. What does that mean for you?", a: ["Removals are paid separately later", "The removals are inside the lump sum, so someone must price them on bid day", "Removals are NIC"], c: 1, why: "K-24-1952 300-1.4 says no other payment will be made, so the cost must be in the bid." },
    { q: "How many in-place CY are in a 900 SF slab, 4 inches thick?", a: ["About 3.7 CY", "About 11.1 CY", "About 33.3 CY"], c: 1, why: "900 × 4 ÷ 324 = 11.1 CY." },
    { q: "You need 180 compacted CY of fill. Shrink factor 0.90, swell 1.25, trucks hold 10 LCY. How many loads?", a: ["18", "20", "25"], c: 2, why: "180 ÷ 0.90 = 200 BCY; × 1.25 = 250 LCY; ÷ 10 = 25 loads." },
    { q: "Why do proposals state a rough grade tolerance like “±0.1 ft of finish grade less mulch and amendment depth”?", a: ["It's required by CSLB", "So excess soil caused by a high rough grade is a change order, not your cost", "To make the proposal longer"], c: 1, why: "Without it, the GC can say fixing the grade was part of your fine grading." },
    { q: "Under the State Water Board's CGP, who writes the SWPPP?", a: ["A QSD", "A QSP", "The landscape foreman"], c: 0, why: "The Qualified SWPPP Developer writes and revises the SWPPP; the QSP carries it out in the field." },
    { q: "On four Caltrans jobs, every bidder priced “Rain Event Action Plan” at $500 each. What does that tell you?", a: ["The bidders copied each other", "The price was set in the bid documents", "It was a math error"], c: 1, why: "Identical prices across all bidders point to a fixed price set by the agency." },
    { q: "Under APCD Rule 55, when must track-out on public roads be removed?", a: ["Once a week", "At the end of each work day (or every 24 hours for continuous work)", "Only when the inspector asks"], c: 1, why: "Rule 55 (d)(2)(ii) requires removal at the end of each work day." },
    { q: "How far ahead must you notify DigAlert before digging?", a: ["At least 2 working days, not more than 14 calendar days", "At least 24 hours", "Any time within 30 days"], c: 0, why: "Gov. Code §4216.2(b); the day you call doesn't count." },
    { q: "On 11-4C0034, what did the landscape company bidding as prime do with the structural concrete wall?", a: ["Self-performed it", "Listed a subcontractor for 100% of the wall items", "Excluded it"], c: 1, why: "Diversified listed Hazard Construction for items 53, 54 and 55. A public prime can't exclude bid items." },
    { q: "The City stabilized DG spec requires compaction tests by a certified lab for each lift. Who pays?", a: ["The City", "The contractor", "The DG supplier"], c: 1, why: "200-5.3.4 says testing is the sole financial responsibility of the Contractor." },
    { q: "On 11-431064 the listed water meter charges were $118,667, and Caltrans adjusts the item if fees change. What's the smart way to price it?", a: ["Carry at least the listed charges plus your handling time", "Bid $0 since it's a pass-through", "Double it for safety"], c: 0, why: "The fee is known and adjusted if it changes; bid at least what you'll pay plus your cost to arrange it." },
    { q: "A GC's scope sheet lists “site lighting” and you didn't price it. What do you write?", a: ["Nothing; they'll figure it out", "“Excluded; by electrical contractor” on that line", "“Included” and hope it's small"], c: 1, why: "Silence reads as included. Exclude it in writing, or sub it with a quote." }
  ]
},

{ t: "practice", id: "m18", h: "Practice assignments",
  lead: "Real documents, real phone calls, and math drills with answers. Do them in order.",
  items: [
    { h: "Read the license texts", hrs: 0.5, d: "<p>On <a href=\"https://www.cslb.ca.gov/About_us/Library/Licensing_Classifications/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CSLB's classification pages</a>, read the C-27, C-8, C-10, C-12, C-13, C-7 and the C-61 subcategories D-6, D-12, D-34 and D-49. For each, write one sentence in your own words and one landscape-bid item it would cover. Then look up two San Diego landscape competitors on CSLB's Instant License Check and note every class they hold.</p>" },
    { h: "Build a responsibility matrix for a real job", hrs: 1, d: "<p>Download a current landscape or park bid from the City of San Diego's <a href=\"https://www.sandiego.gov/cip/bidopps\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">bid opportunities</a> page, or use a GC invitation you received. List every scope that isn't irrigation or planting (demo, grading, BMPs, drainage, hardscape, furnishings, lighting, utilities, general conditions). For each, fill in: typically by, landscape sub yes/no, license, and your answer (In, Sub, Out, NIC). Cite the sheet or spec section for each line.</p>" },
    { h: "Call for real demo and haul rates", hrs: 0.75, d: "<p>Call or check the websites of one concrete and asphalt recycler, one landfill or transfer station (for example the City's Miramar Landfill), and one trucking company in San Diego County. Write down: price per ton or load for clean concrete, clean soil, green waste and mixed debris; truck hourly rate and minimum; hours. Then redo the patio demo worked example with your real numbers and compare the total.</p>" },
    { h: "Earthwork and hardscape math drills", hrs: 0.5, d: "<p>Show every line. (1) In-place CY, tons (2 tons per CY) and 10-LCY loads (swell 1.50) for 1,200 SF of 6\" concrete. (2) Loads of import for 150 CCY of fill, shrink 0.90, swell 1.25, 10 LCY trucks. (3) Export loads to lower 5,000 SF by 2\", swell 1.25. (4) CY for 250 LF of 6\"×6\" mow curb plus 10% waste. (5) Stabilized DG for 900 SF at 4\": compacted CY, loose CY at +20%, tons at 1.4, binder pounds at 12 lb per ton. (6) Number of 25-ft fiber rolls for 2,000 LF. Answers: (1) 22.2 CY, 44.4 tons, 33.3 LCY → 4 loads; (2) 166.7 BCY, 208.3 LCY → 21 loads; (3) 30.9 BCY, 38.6 LCY → 4 loads; (4) 2.31 CY → 2.55 CY; (5) 11.1, 13.3 CY, 18.7 tons, 224 lb; (6) 80 rolls.</p>" },
    { h: "Price the BMPs on a real Caltrans job", hrs: 0.75, d: "<p>Open <a href=\"#lab-11-428704\">Real Bid Lab · 11-428704</a>. Read special provisions section 13 (risk level 2, WPC manager, SWPPP by a QSD). Price items 10 through 17 yourself with example costs, including maintenance. Then compare each item to the six bidders' unit prices and write two sentences on which items had fixed prices and why your numbers differ.</p>" },
    { h: "Take off stabilized DG from a real spec", hrs: 0.5, d: "<p>Using the City of San Diego stabilized DG section quoted in this module (200-5: sterilant, 90% subgrade, two 2\" lifts, 12 lb binder per ton minimum, 1/8\" in 10' tolerance, lab tests per lift), take off a 2,400 SF path with 320 LF of edging. List quantities, every submittal and test, and the inspection holds. Price it with example rates, showing the math.</p>" },
    { h: "Write a subcontractor quote request", hrs: 0.5, d: "<p>Write a quote request email to a C-10 electrical contractor for controller power and landscape lighting on the job you used in assignment 2. Include: documents and addenda, exact scope by sheet and detail, what you will provide (controller location, sleeves), prevailing wage or not, bid date, and what you need back (license number, DIR number if public, exclusions, lead times). Add a markup line to your estimate showing how you'll carry their price.</p>" },
    { h: "Build your other-scopes proposal template", hrs: 0.5, d: "<p>Turn the reusable checklist in this module into a one-page template in your proposal file (Module 13). For each scope, keep one “included” and one “excluded” sentence you're comfortable using. Fill it out for the job from assignment 2.</p>" },
    { h: "DigAlert and locating checklist", hrs: 0.25, d: "<p>Read Government Code §§4216.2 and 4216.4 on <a href=\"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">leginfo.legislature.ca.gov</a> and visit <a href=\"https://digalert.org\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">digalert.org</a>. Write a 10-line field checklist: delineate in white, ticket timing, ticket renewal at 28 days, tolerance zone hand digging, private line locating, and what to do when a line is hit. Add a line to your estimate template for potholing and private locating.</p>" },
    { h: "Who subbed what on 11-4C0034", hrs: 0.25, d: "<p>Using the Scenario 3 table, list each prime's subcontracted scopes. Calculate the wall items' share of the low bid: ($385,000 + $7,200 + $22,700) ÷ $1,597,008.50. Answer: $414,900 ÷ $1,597,008.50 = 26.0%. Write three sentences on whether your division should bid a job like this as prime, and what you'd need first.</p>" }
  ]
},

{ h: "Module 18 summary",
  lead: "You can now find every non-landscape scope in a bid, decide what to do with it, price what you keep and exclude the rest in writing.",
  body: `<ul>
<li>Landscape packages carry demo, grading, BMPs, drainage, hardscape, furnishings, lighting, utilities and general conditions. Read the scope sheet, specs and bid items for all of them.</li>
<li>The C-27 covers landscape systems and grading <em>for</em> landscape. Walls, line-voltage electrical, mass grading, storm drain and playgrounds usually need other classes: sub them or exclude them.</li>
<li>Demo: SF × inches ÷ 324 = in-place CY; × swell = loose CY for trucks; about 2 tons per CY of concrete for dump fees. Watch for demo buried inside other items.</li>
<li>Earthwork: BCY, LCY and CCY are different. State the rough grade you expect to receive in every proposal.</li>
<li>Storm water: the GC or prime owns the SWPPP (QSD/QSP). Price BMP upkeep, not just install. Rule 55 governs dust and track-out.</li>
<li>Drainage, DG, walls and furnishings: read the detail and spec for testing, binder, lifts, footings and submittals. Small jobs don't get big-job unit prices.</li>
<li>Utilities: meter fees can be huge pass-throughs; sleeves go in before paving; DigAlert 2 working days ahead; hand dig in the 24-inch tolerance zone.</li>
<li>Bid day: answer every line (In, Sub, Out, NIC), mark up subs, and never decline by silence.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Next: use this module with Module 13 · Writing the Proposal and Module 15 · Real Bid Practice.</strong> Links: <a href=\"#m13-1\">Module 13</a> · <a href=\"#m15-1\">Module 15</a> · <a href=\"#lab\">Real Bid Lab</a></p>`
}
]
});
